{"parentUuid":"4733edf2-8070-411a-969b-4c2c039a55da","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_012cSL4RE1UZhR5DJB3XhNBo","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5c431f71-17e7-4d1a-82d2-0aae3208faf9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:39:15.369Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"45168860-27b1-4ba5-b3de-30fc3d5649b3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012cSL4RE1UZhR5DJB3XhNBo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t######## PDF 252 / p.241\n3\tSUPERTRAINING\n4\tThere is an ancient injunction that the weavers of Eastern carpet must include at least one flaw in their \n5\twork to recognisc that perfection is reserved for the Divine and not for humans. The above findings imply that \n6\tijnilar asymmetry or lack of perfection may be desirable for all human physical performance and training.\n7\t4.2.9 The Concept o f M uscle Isolation\n8\tGenerations of muscle magazine authors have extolled the virtues of special ways of training to isolate specific \n9\tmuscle groups. They have advocated certain machines, ways of fixating the joints, precise placement of the limbs \n10\tud numerous other ingenious manoeuvres to ensure that one muscle is maximally recruited while others remain \n11\tlargely dormant.\n12\tEMG studies reveal that isolation of minute muscle groups is indeed possible under low intensities of \n13\tloading (e.g. Basmajian, 1978). Intuitively, we are aware that low intensity actions such as eye-blinking, piano- \n14\tplaying or relaxed speaking are due to the precise action of specific muscle groups, a fact which the EMG clearly \n15\tcorroborates.\n16\tHowever, if large loads are imposed on a given muscle group by intense resistance, isolation becomcs virtu- \n17\tilly impossible. Immediately the load is applied, stabilising muscles become involved to ensure that the body or \n18\tspecific joints remain stable, while the prime movers attempt to cope with the load. If the nervous system signals \n19\tthat the prime movers are inadequate to execute the desired joint action, assistant movers, shunt muscles, \n20\temergency muscles and neutralises may all be recruited to augment the action of the prime movers.\n21\tPhysiotherapists relying on PNF techniques sometimes impose maximal loading on a joint to elicit \n22\tcompensatory or overflow activity o f muscles other than the ‘prime movers.* \n23\tThis is not possible if the \n24\tresistance imposed is inadequate to overload the ‘prime movers.* Similarly, the weight trainer who wishes to \n25\tincrease hypertrophy or strength of a given muscle selects a weight which forces it to operate at high intensity. \n26\tHie higher the intensity or the greater the duration of the exercise, the less likely is isolation of the prime mover, \n27\teven if certain joints arc restrained as on an isokinetic test machine.\n28\tIn many cases the tension developed in the stabilisers or assistant stabilisers will equal or exceed that of the \n29\tpnme movers. For example, during heavy standing tricep press-downs with cable machines, the abdominal \n30\tmuscles, latissimus dorsi and erector spinae of the back may be as strongly contracted as the triceps, even though \n31\tthis is supposedly an isolation exercise for the triceps. Analysis of many seated or reclining isolation exercises on \n32\tmachines reveals that isolation simply docs not occur with heavy loads.\n33\tThis is not to negate the value of so-called isolation exercises. Not only can they be useful during the early \n34\tstages of injury rehabilitation, but overflow to other muscle groups may be very desirable (if the exercise is \n35\tperformed safely), sincc it simultaneously offers isometric and dynamic training to many other muscle groups. It \n36\ttsnecessary, however, to clarify the popular misconceptions surrounding the misleading concept of muscle \n37\tisolation still proliferated by numerous instructors and in many training texts.\n38\t4.3 Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of S trength \n39\tTraining\n40\tDevising methods of special strength training is associated firstly with the selection of the means and methods for \n41\tdeveloping muscular strength. \n42\tAH sporting movement is specific and goal-directed. Therefore, the strength \n43\tdisplayed in the execution of each movement is also specific and goal-directed. One should not refer to strength in \n44\tgeneral, but only in the context of the given task. In other words, the means and methods of strength training \n45\tshould provide an adequate conditioning regime for the motor system in the special exercises and thereby ensure \n46\tcontinued improvement in sporting performance.\n47\tThe foundation of this assertion lies in the principle o f dynamic correspondence, which emphasizes that the \n48\tmeans and methods of strength training for specific sports should be chosen to enhance the required motor \n49\tqualities in terms o f :\n50\t• The amplitude and direction of movement\n51\t• The accentuated region of force production\n52\t• The dynamics o f the effort\n53\t• The rate and time of maximum force production\n54\t• The regime of muscular work\n55\t241\n56\t\n57\t######## PDF 253 / p.242\n58\tSUPERTRAINING\n59\t4.3.1 The Amplitude and D irection of M ovement\n60\tThe criterion of correspondence with respect to the amplitude and direction originates from the patterns of the \n61\tmovement relative to the adjacent body part. It determines which muscles are involved in the work and allows for \n62\tanatomical peculiarities and the external conditions of work. Thus, the movement of the shoulder girdle of a row« \n63\tor shot putter is about the same in amplitude, but the direction of the working force is different (Fig 4.17). In the \n64\tfirst case the pulling of the oars involves shoulder extension, whereas in the second case it involves shoulder \n65\tflexion acting in opposition to the force of inertia of the shot.\n66\tThe importance of the correspondence of muscular work, in terms of the relevant criteria may be illustrated \n67\tby the following example. In track-and-field training, jumpers and sprinters sometimes do exercises to develop the \n68\thip flexor muscles in a standing position on a hip flexor machine which offers resistance by means of a pad \n69\tpressing against the front of the thigh.\n70\tHowever, the amplitude of thigh movement in running and jumping (when moving the legs forward) ij \n71\tsignificantly greater than the amplitude of this exercise, and begins at an angle of about 210° in the hip joint, \n72\trelative to the torso. Therefore, the execution of this exercise in the standing position docs not duplicate the \n73\tmcchanism of the movement in the sport specific exercise ( e.g. jumping or sprinting).\n74\tFigure 4.17 Working amplitude of shoulder movement of a rower and a shotputter (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n75\tIf the athlete’s position changes (Fig 4.18), the criterion of the correspondence is met, not only with respect to the \n76\tamplitude of the movement, but also to the sporting movement, because the resistance imitates the inertial \n77\tresistance of the mass of the leg rotating about the hip joint in the special exercise. By altering the load, the \n78\tnumber of repetitions and the tempo of movement, the task of developing the strength to produce the required \n79\tforce and strength-endurance, will be solved. Thus, to fulfill the criteria of correspondence with respect to the \n80\tamplitude and direction of movement, it is advisable to select the exact starting position and posture of the ath­\n81\tlete, as well as to calculate the direction of action of the forces associated with the working links of the system \n82\tand the additional load.\n83\tThe line of action of the applied external resistance and of the loaded movement as a whole must also be \n84\ttaken into account. For example, in middle-distancc running, skiing and skating, a knapsack full o f sand ora \n85\tweight belt are sometimes used as resistance. However, the muscles which bear the load are those which resist the \n86\tweight o f the body. This can increase the ability to cope with vertical loading and develop general strength- \n87\tendurance, but docs not strengthen those muscles which propel the body horizontally.\n88\tFigure 4.18 Exercise for developing hip flexion strength based on consideration for the accentuated part o f the working amplitude and \n89\tthe resistance to the force of muscle contraction (SiflF & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n90\tSimilarly, a skater may exccute jumps on one leg on the floor or from a bench. These exercises strengthen the leg \n91\tmuscles supporting the body and the static-endurance of the back muscles, but do not fully imitate the working of \n92\tthe muscles for the push-off, where the force is directed backward.\n93\t242\n94\t\n95\t######## PDF 254 / p.243\n96\tSUPERTRAINING\n97\tSkaters should use another method of resisted movement by changing the direction in which the force of \n98\tresistance is acting (Fig 4.19). These methods to a large extent match the training exercise to the dynamics of the \n99\tsport specific actions.\n100\tFigure 4.19 Training means for increasing resistance during skating (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\n101\tIn sport, the working force is developed by the simultaneous coordinated tension of the muscle groups which \n102\tmove the various segments of the body. The most characteristic example of such cooperation of the working \n103\tmuscles is the simultaneous flexion and extension at the two hip joints (e.g. in running, jumping, fencing, figure \n104\tskating, basketball or tennis), where the angular movement o f one leg enhances the push-off movement of the \n105\tother. It is useful to imitate this combination with special strength exercises which take into account the working \n106\tamplitude of the movement and the direction of the opposing force (as in Fig 4.20). On the other hand, it is \n107\tusually inappropriate to perform the actual sporting movements with significantly large added loads in an attempt \n108\tto provide exact simulation, since the added loading invariably alters the centres of gravity and rotation of the \n109\tlimbs and body, thereby altering the underlying motor patterns and dispositions of joints relative to one another.\n110\tFigure 4.20 Complex exercise for simultaneous strengthening of the hip flexors and extensors, and knee extensors (Verkhoshansky, \n111\t1977).\n112\tParachute R esistance T raining\n113\tSometimes, small parachutes of different size (to offer different degrees of resistance) may be attached with a \n114\tquick-releasc harness to the trunk of the athlete to offer a low-inertia form of resistance (Popov, 1966). Although \n115\tthere have been claims that these parachutes were developed secretly in Moscow in 1988 (Brunner & Tabachnik \n116\tSoviet Training and Recovery Methods 1990), this reference shows that Popov and others were researching their \n117\tuse more than 20 years earlier.\n118\tThe quick release may be used to decrease the resistance rapidly while the athlete is running and thereby offer \n119\tthe benefits of contrast training, such as acceleration strength (see 7.1). Due largely to the efforts of emigre \n120\tRussians such as Dr Ben Tabachnik, a former soviet track coach now living in the USA, these running parachutes \n121\thave become a popular training aid in the West, where they generally have been applied fairly randomly with little \n122\tknowledge of pcriodising them optimally into overall training programmes. Appropriateness of use depends on \n123\tan understanding of which strength qualities are required by the athlete at a given stage of training.\n124\tIn particular, parachute training plays no role in the development of starting strength, which is produced \n125\tunder isometric conditions. They, however, offer a safer alternative to the use of weights added to the extremities\n126\t243\n127\t\n128\t######## PDF 255 / p.244\n129\tSUPERTRAINING\n130\t244\n131\tor body, or dragged behind the athlete (e.g. a car tyre or sled), because they do not increase the vertical reaction \n132\tforces acting on the body. The fact that the parachutes offer aerodynamic resistance which increases rapidly with \n133\tvelocity (actually the square of the velocity) allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably. Thus, smaller \n134\tparachutes are more suited for speed development, medium parachutes for speed-endurance and large parachutes \n135\tfor specd-strcngth and acceleration-strength. Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance, so \n136\tthat optimal development of these fitness or motor qualities depends on attempts to run at maximum speed for as \n137\tlong as possible and implementation of various fartlck or interval methods. The drag force characteristically varies \n138\tbetween 5 - 200 Newtons for running speeds of 6 -10 metres per second, depending on the size of the parachute \n139\t(Zatsiorsky, 1995).\n140\tQuick release of the parachute at maximum velocity can be helpful as a special contrast method in enhancing \n141\tspeed and stride frequency. Training distances commonly vary between 30m and 150m, with smaller parachutes \n142\tbeing used for the longer distances. If the basic principles of its application arc clearly understood, parachute \n143\ttraining can be used successfully by distance runners, as well as sprint or distance cyclists and swimmers. As \n144\twith other resistance training, this method must be applied carefully so as not to disrupt motor skill and \n145\tcoordination. In particular, it should be noted that parachutes offer the same degree o f resistance during both the \n146\tsupport and non-support phases of running, thereby disrupting speed and acceleration of limb and body \n147\tmovement during flight. \n148\tIn addition, parachutes alter the relative position of the joints during landing, especially \n149\tduring hurdling and jumping. \n150\tAll of this can adversely affect several aspects of the degree o f dynamic \n151\tcorrespondence of the training means to the actual sporting movements (as discussed throughout Ch 4.3).\n152\tIn using parachutes, the principle of gradual progressive overloading should be applied, with larger parachutes \n153\tbeing used over a period of weeks. In doing so, it is essential to periodically decrease the loading in any given \n154\tworkout or microcycle and to apply the sudden release contrast method to enhance the training effect (see 1.16.3). \n155\tIn any workout, it is common to progress rapidly after the warm-up to the use of large resistance parachutes, then \n156\tconclude with a few repetitions using smaller parachutes.\n157\tTo ensure that this aerodynamic resistance training does not leave after-effects which disturb one's normal \n158\tneuromuscular skills, it is advisable to precede and follow all parachute sessions with the same exercises \n159\tperformed without parachutes. During the preparatory weekly microcycles, parachute sessions typically are \n160\tperformed two or three times a week on alternate days between other types of training session. They arc used \n161\tmore frequently nearer the later stage of the preparatory phase immediately preceding the competitive season.\n162\t4.3.2 The A ccentuated Region of Force Production\n163\tMuscular effort changes during the course of every movement and maximum force is developed at the most \n164\tappropriate instant for a given action. In ballistic movements this instant corresponds to the beginning part of the \n165\tworking amplitude and in movements of mixed regimes of muscular work, this takes place at the instant of \n166\tswitching from one regime to another (Verkhoshansky, 1977). Thus, the working amplitude always has an \n167\taccentuated region where the maximum dynamic force occurs close to a specific joint angle. Based on this, the \n168\tcriterion of correspondence includes the need to produce the required force at a specific joint angle.\n169\tIn the previous example it should be noted that the accentuated region of the working amplitude of the leg \n170\tmovement is near the beginning, as discussed earlier and as shown in the graph of hip flexion torque in the air (Fig \n171\t4.21).\n172\t\n173\t######## PDF 256 / p.245\n174\tSUPERTRAINING\n175\tConsequently, the athletes referred to earlier who run with loads or use hip flexor machines regularly not \n176\tonly do not reproduce the required full amplitude of the movement, but also lose the ability to train the muscles \n177\tto generate the necessary force at the appropriate hip joint angle. This clcarly illustrates the importance of the \n178\tstarting position for executing the special strength exercises that have a local effect on the motor system. The \n179\tstrength exercise should not only reproduce the full amplitude of the movement but also the specific direction of \n180\tresistance to the pull of the muscles.\n181\tFollowing these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines \n182\twhich are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which \n183\tfails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria. In example illustrated, the partner seated on the athlete's \n184\tshoulders simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes, while the partner pulling the back leg \n185\toffers resistance intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors. While this exercise may be appropriate if \n186\tapplied carefully, incorrect positioning or imposition of resistance can reduce its effectiveness markedly.\n187\tFlgirt 4.22 A special strength exercise intended to simulate the flexion-extension action of the hip during sports activities, where the \n188\tKccntuated regions o f force production can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held (Siff & Verkhoshansky, \n189\t1999).\n190\tIt is possible to develop force at the required joint angles with isometric exercises, because they can have a \n191\tparticular value in this respect (see 4.2.4). With isometrics or quasi-isometrics over a given range of movement \n192\tone can locally influence certain muscle groups by paying particular attention to the accentuated regions of the \n193\tmovements where they arc involved. Therefore, the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle \n194\tit which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise.\n195\t4.3.3 The Dynam ics of the Effort\n196\tThe criterion of dynamic effort is the quantitative correspondence of the dynamics of the training means to the \n197\tspecific sports movement. This criterion states that the intensity of the training stimulus should not be less than \n198\tthat encountered in the given exercise at the corresponding level of sports proficiency and should even excced it. \n199\tIn other words, the effort exerted in training should not be less than the effort generated in the specific sports \n200\tmovement. One must now ask whether the maximum value or the mean value should be considered as the \n201\tcriterion of correspondence of strength.\n202\tDepending upon external conditions, a person can display maximal strength in two forms: in the movement \n203\tofheavy loads or light loads. The maximum force can actually be larger in the sccond case than in the first, since \n204\tthe lighter load can be rapidly accelerated (remembering that F = ma). However, it is unnecessary to exercise only \n205\twith light loads, even if the maximum achieved corresponds closely to the dynamics of the special exercise. It is \n206\timportant to consider first the duration and character of the effort.\n207\tThe fact is that the external force displayed in these two cases is qualitatively different: in the first, \n208\tmaximum force is determined by the absolute strength of the muscles, and in the sccond by the speed o f \n209\tcontraction. Therefore, the strength and training effects of the respective movements are also different.\n210\tFrom this it follows that, in striving to generate maximal effort in training, the athlete should become clearly \n211\tacquainted with the type of strength which is needed and satisfy the criteria of correspondence according to the \n212\timount of effort and the movement time for the specific exercise. In other words, correspondence with respect to \n213\tthe maximum and mean magnitude of strength can be determined only by calculating the speed of movement. If \n214\tthe athlete finds the means for achieving this, he can be confident that he is on the right track in organising his \n215\tspecial strength training.\n216\t245\n217\t\n218\t######## PDF 257 / p.246\n219\tSUPERTRAINING\n220\t2 4 6\n221\tThe following examples serve to clarify the issue. If the special exercise requires the athlete to overcome I \n222\tlarge resistance at a relatively low speed, then training should be aimed primarily at developing maximal strength. \n223\tConversely, if the athlete has to deal with a small resistance in the special exercise and execute the movement u \n224\tmaximal speed, then, according to the criterion of correspondence, he should apply a moderate amount of effort \n225\tand pay particular attention to the duration of the movement.\n226\t4.3.4 The Rate and Tim e of Force Production\n227\tThe criterion of correspondence regarding the rate of developing maximum force (see 2.2.1) complements the \n228\tcriterion of dynamic effort and is particularly important in those sporting activities requiring explosive strength. \n229\tWe have already stated that the strength displayed in training should be examined by taking into account its rate \n230\tof production, which is equivalent to measuring the time taken to execute the movement. The production of \n231\texplosive strength implicates a brief-acting moderate force whose characteristics may be described in terms of \n232\tfeatures such as the power and the impulse (F. Al) of the force, where s is the displacement and At is the brief \n233\ttime interval over which the force F acts (sec 5.1). The following factors are relevant in this context:\n234\tHere W refers to the work and may be defined in its integral form asW - jF(t).ds where s is the displacement \n235\tproduced by the force F. Thus, if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain:\n236\tThe right hand side of this equation quite simply reduces to the familiar basic expression for power \n237\tPower \n238\tP = F.V \n239\t.....where V is the velocity of the object\n240\tUnfortunately, this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to I \n241\tserious misunderstanding o f training theory and methods. If the force is not constant over the time period being I \n242\tanalysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1) which reminds us to consider how \n243\tthe force and velocity (as functions of time) vary over the time interval concerned. This can be done most \n244\tconveniently by examining graphs of F versus V (e.g. Figs 3.15 - 3.18), F versus time and V versus time (e.g. Figs \n245\t2.13,3.3, 3.4,3.14, 3.27,4.7).\n246\tIt is especially important to be aware o f how the force-time (i.e. the RFD or Rate of Force Development) \n247\tcurve changes with time (i.e., the slope of the F-t curve, shown in Fig 2.13). It is inadequate to refer to average or \n248\tpeak force alone. The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD I \n249\tfactor (which was introduced in 2.2.1 as the factor that best defines explosive strength). This conclusion is vital \n250\tto the design of strength training programmes to improve performance in any sport which involves explosive I \n251\tactions. The emergence of plyometric training as an adjunct method of special strength training is one of the \n252\tpractical consequences of understanding the implications of the preceding analysis of power as a motor ability.\n253\tIn all plyometric actions, the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot \n254\tbe regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase. In such cases it is better to rely on a force plate or high \n255\tspeed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations \n256\tthat will inevitably produce grave errors.\n257\tThe other factor used to analyse explosive strength, the Reactivity Coefficient, was defined earlier (2.2.1) as the \n258\tRFD relative to one's bodyweight (or the weight of any object being moved rapidly):\n259\tReactivity Coefficient \n260\tRC = Fmax/ tmax.mg = RFDmax^W\n261\twhere Fmax is the maximum force exerted, tmax is the time taken to reach this maximum, the mass is m and g is the \n262\tacceleration due to gravity = 9.8m/s2.\n263\tThe above equations may be used to evaluate explosive strength, although the significance of each of them can \n264\tbe different, depending on conditions. Thus, evaluation using calculations of the impulse of the force is acceptable \n265\tonly when it is not necessary to compare different movements. If such a need arises, then the evaluation is valid \n266\tonly if the time taken is identical for each movement, a situation that does not occur in human movement. \n267\tTherefore, in this particular case, determination of the impulse of the force is irrelevant.\n268\t\n269\t######## PDF 258 / p.247\n270\tSUPERTRAININC\n271\tIn those cases where the working effort is displayed over a brief time interval, a necessary condition of the \n272\tmovement’s effectiveness is the speed with which maximum effort is developed. In other words, if it is necessary \n273\tfor the athlete to produce a large force within some time limit, he must do it quickly and use a high RFD. Such a \n274\tnecessity is dictated by the conditions of the activity and by the biomechanical characteristics of the \n275\tmusculoskeletal system, especially the working amplitude of the movement.\n276\tThus, the RFD, or rate with which the required maximum force is developed (determined from the slope of \n277\tthe force-time curve), is an important criterion of correspondence for resolving the task of special strength training \n278\t(sec 2.2.1), which relates directly to the characteristics of the neuromuscular system. This again emphasizes the \n279\tfact that all training is primarily neuromuscular and that training based entirely on variables such as exercise \n280\tchoice, load, sets and repetitions is highly inadequate for producing sport specific fitness.\n281\tIt should be stressed that fulfillment of the criterion of correspondence regarding strength and the speed with \n282\twhich it achieves a maximum creates its own unique problem, the successful resolution of which depends upon \n283\tthe efficiency of the neuromuscular training process.\n284\t4.3.5 The Regim e of Muscular Work\n285\tThe criterion of correspondence regarding the sports regime relies on determining the character of the muscular \n286\twork involved. In particular, the regime of muscular work should be taken into consideration for selecting the \n287\tmeans and methods of special strength training. \n288\tThe fact is that, depending upon the character of its execution, \n289\tthe same means can solve different tasks. For example, boxers and shot putters need to execute the same exercise \n290\t(e.g. performing a thrusting arm movement against the resistance of a pulley machine) with different weights, \n291\ttempos, number of sets and repetitions, because boxing requires primarily a quick, unresisted movement and the \n292\tability to repeat it many times without significant decrease in force, whereas shotputting involves rapid single \n293\tmovements against a specific resistance.\n294\tThe selection of a regime of muscular work is difficult where a specific motor action is concerned. However, \n295\tit is even more difficult in all-round sports like decathlon, gymnastics and modem pentathlon. Therefore, the \n296\tproblem of selecting a regime of muscular work involves two obvious tasks:\n297\t• selection of the regime for a specific motor action (e.g. the key elements in an exercise)\n298\t• selection of the main regime which will best develop all of the diverse muscular activities in all-round training.\n299\tThe latter issue is still far from being resolved, although the evolution of training methods in sport indicates that it \n300\tshould be more thoroughly understood in the near future.\n301\tAlmost one hundred years of experience in training all-round track athletes indicates that their fundamental \n302\ttraining regime is speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type. This was corroborated by \n303\tusing statistical analysis to identify the key exercises in the 'all-round' complex (Zatsiorsky, 1966; Lukauskas, \n304\t1967; Mamdzhanyan, 1976). To corroborate this idea one should examine the preparation of gymnasts. For a long \n305\ttime they adhered to primarily static training, with many of them including some dynamic exercises such as \n306\tjumping. The progress of competitive standards and the necessity to master complex elements such as jumping \n307\twith extensive longitudinal rotation compelled them to examine the value of the cyclic work regime and reconsider \n308\tit in favour of dynamic muscular tension.\n309\tThus, it may be concluded that cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably should be regarded as the most \n310\timportant in all-round training. It is also necessary to add other regimes, determined by the motor skills required \n311\tby the specific sport. However, this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\n312\tIn conjunction with perfecting motor skills in a specific activity, one should consider the importance of \n313\tchanging from one activity to another in those exercises where this is necessary. Thus, an athlete's results in the \n314\tlong jump are determined mainly by the ability to switch from the cyclic regime of muscular work in running, to \n315\texplosive effort in the take-off. For instance, good gymnasts are capable of switching rapidly from dynamic \n316\texplosive effort to isometric tension. The methodology underlying the development of these complex capabilities \n317\tis the aim of technical training in all sport. Its resolution is the goal of special strength training.\n318\t4.3.6 Correspondence of Training Means to the Sports M ovem ents\n319\tThe scientific understanding of the character of muscular work and motor strength is derived from analysis of the \n320\tathlete's movements, i.e. the entire complex of interacting motor actions which resolve the motor tasks most\n321\t247\n322\t\n323\t######## PDF 259 / p.248\n324\tSUPERTRAINING\n325\teffectively. The most convenient way o f doing this is to use a high speed video system to record and analyse \n326\teach movement at intervals separated by fractions of a second.\n327\tThe kinematic structure characterises the interrelation of the separate movements in space and time and \n328\thelps to identify those actions which play either a fundamental or supportive role in determining motor skill. \n329\tFrom here it is easy to take one further step to determine the means of perfecting the movements and especially \n330\tthe means and methods of developing functional strength. Naturally, knowledge of the movement will be more \n331\tprecise if it is based on quantitative information which can be obtained only from computerised video analysis.\n332\tThe analysis of the kinematics of a motor action to model its mechanisms can be based on scrutiny of the \n333\tphasic structure of the movement (according to Donskoi). The act as a whole or its elements can always be \n334\tdivided into separate phases in which the direction of movement, the application of force and the character of \n335\tmuscular work are distinguished. The interrelation between separate phases enables one to gain some idea of the \n336\tmuscle actions and the movement as a whole, particularly during critical phases of the task. Such a representation \n337\twill be more complete if forces are simultaneously recorded along with the video images (e.g. using a force plate or \n338\tcable tensiometer), thereby permitting the total interaction of the person with the external objects to be analysed.\n339\tAs has already been stated, full reproduction of the complex interaction of the different types o f fitness \n340\tstrength is not always possible in training. It becomes necessary to exercise locally the interacting muscle groups \n341\tthat are crucial for controlling specific movements in a sporting task, which suggests that the training load be \n342\tincreased. Such exercise should be based on the characteristics for fonning and developing the kincsiological \n343\tstructure of the motor action (see 2.1.2, 2.1.3). It has been established that, in the course of mastering a motor \n344\tact, the elements of the kinesiological structure develop in a particular pattern of heterochronicity, depending on \n345\ttheir origin and manner of interdependence (see 2.2.3).\n346\tThe development of some elements is determined by their direct interaction with external objects, whereas \n347\tothers arc determined by indirect interaction with these objects, so that they develop at a slower rate. An \n348\tindispensable condition for forming and developing the biodynamic structure is the relatively harmonious \n349\tdevelopment of its elements. This task can be successfully undertaken only by means of an organised system of \n350\tspecial strength training.\n351\tThus, it must be emphasized again that success in selecting the means of special strength training is \n352\tdetermined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements.\n353\t4.4 Strength Training and General Endurance\n354\tThe scientifically corroborated concepts conccming energy production for muscular work play an important role \n355\tin solving the practical problems of training. Analysis of the functional specialisation of the body solely at the \n356\tlevel of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate, especially if the physiology of endurance \n357\tis considered with respect to the respiratory functions and maximum oxygen consumption (VO2 max) alone. Since \n358\texisting regimes of endurance training are still based on such ideas, improvement of modem methods of endurance \n359\tdevelopment is now particularly urgent. This question is examined in detail below:\n360\t1. Athletes who have equivalent V02 max levels show different results, and conversely, athletes with different \n361\tlevels o f aerobic efficicncy demonstrate the same results. For example, top middle-distance runners of the \n362\t1940s had the same VO2 max as today's athletes, yet there are major differences in performance (Faulkner, \n363\t1968). There arc no statistically reliable differences between athletes of different qualification regarding the \n364\tdevelopment of aerobic potential, although there are significant differences with respect to anaerobic effi­\n365\tciency (Nabotnikova, 1972; Serafimova, 1974; Rusko, 1976).\n366\t2. The VO2 max levels in highly-qualified athletes stabilises, but results continue to improve. For example, over \n367\ta 4-5 year period the V02 max levels of the best Soviet and Swedish skaters stabilised, but their results still \n368\timproved from year to year (Orlov & Sharova, 1977). V02 max levels remained practically unchanged in \n369\tqualified road cyclists over many years of training, yet their performances improved (Melenberg et al, 1972).\n370\t3. A significant dccrcasc in V02 max levels has been observed during the competition period, especially in \n371\tcyclists (Bakhvalov, 1974; Zatsiorsky et al, 1974), swimmers (Scrafimov, 1974; Volkov ct al, 1974; \n372\tNaumenko, 1978), skiers (Vasilicv & Trunin, 1974; Zima ct al, 1975), skaters (Stenin, 1973; Vavilov, 1977), \n373\tmiddlc-distance runners (Loktyev, 1978, Borisov, 1979; Sirenko, 1981) and hockey players (Koloskov, \n374\t1976). There is a decreasing correlation between V02 max and improving sport results. The correlation\n375\t248\n376\t\n377\t######## PDF 260 / p.249\n378\tSUPERTRAINING\n379\tcoefficient is R = 0.7 during the preparation period, whereas it is 0.4 during the competitive period (Vasiliev \n380\t& Trunin, 1974; Melenberg, 1981).\n381\tThis data indicates that V02 max on its own is no guarantee of outstanding performance. It is now recognised \n382\tthat, apart from aerobic power, other factors play a role, such as the ability to perform work for a prolonged \n383\tperiod at a level of oxygen consumption close to the V02 max, and the ability to effectively utilise available \n384\tenergy under competitive conditions. This concerns the decrease in V02 max during the competition period, a \n385\tphenomenon possibly explained by the decreasing trainability of aerobic processes or by an inherent antagonism \n386\tbetween aerobic and anaerobic processes (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Volkov, 1975).\n387\tOxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\n388\tPractical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential \n389\tto utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of \n390\toxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated. Anaerobic work is \n391\tapproximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's \n392\tadvantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include \n393\tmaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950).\n394\tSince OBLA depends on V02 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to \n395\tcreate some reserve of aerobic power by the end o f the preparatory period to avoid a decrease in V02 max during \n396\tthe competition period, which may lower the specific work-capacity. Considering that a significant amount of the \n397\tenergy production of intense muscular work is achieved by recruiting the glycolytic mechanism of ATP \n398\tresynthesis, it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration.\n399\tHowever, recent research indicates that an increase in endurance is associated more with enhancing the \n400\tability of the musclcs to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the \n401\timount of oxygen in the bloodstream and improving the oxygen supply to the working muscles (Ekblom, 1969; \n402\tRowell, 1971; Sal tin, 1974). Consequently, it is not simply the magnitude of V02 max that determines endurance, \n403\tbut intramuscular factors which facilitate adaptation of the muscles to prolonged intense work. A rise in \n404\tmtramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the \n405\tmuscles decrease the rate of glycolysis (Newshalme & Randle, 1964; Saltin & Karlsson, 1971; Paul et al, 1966; \n406\tGnmby et al, 1967). At the same time, there is a significant drop in the end products of metabolism, including the \n407\tspeed of oxidation of lactate in the working muscles (Jorfeldt, 1970). It is in the skeletal muscles and not in the \n408\tliver and myocardium, as has always been believed, where there is a drop in lactate during and after work \n409\t(Jorfeldt, 1970; Knutten, 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen, 1973).\n410\tThus, the development o f endurance is associated with functional specialisation o f the skeletal muscles, \n411\tparticularly the enhancement o f their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement o f \n412\tcardiorespiratory ability. Consequently, the fundamental emphasis in developing endurance should be to decrease \n413\tthe proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise \n414\tlactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate. In other \n415\twords, in addition to utilising the aerobic pathway of energy production with maximum efficiency, the develop­\n416\tment of endurance should be aimed chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities \n417\tof the muscles, which is the main reason for a high lactate concentration. Recently, haemodynamic factors have \n418\tbeen shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work \n419\t(Vasiliev, 1970, 1974; Ozolin & Partsik, 1970; Danilov, 1980; Kurbanov, 1977; Rowell, 1971; Kaijser, 1970; \n420\tforlsson, 1971; Gollnick et al, 1975). The redistribution o f blood and an increase in circulation to the working \n421\tnuscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements o f the muscles and the removal o f anaerobic metabolites.\n422\tDifferentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow and an optimal \n423\tblood supply to the working muscles takes place during the competition period as a result of the extensive aerobic \n424\tloading executed during the preparatory period. For example, research has shown that improvement in cycling \n425\tperformance (in 25 km road racing) is accompanied by a decrease in V02 max during the competition period, while \n426\tpeak blood flow to the lower extremities increases. At the same time, cardiac output decreases during the \n427\tcompetition period, a measure which reflects the efficiency of the cardiovascular system (Stepochkin et al, 1970). \n428\tThe very same conditions which create economy of aerobic energy production decrease the involvement of \n429\tglycolytic processes and, consequently, lessen dependence on V02 max. In this way, it is possible to eliminate \n430\tthe basic cause of the decrease in VO2 max during the competition period. If this is so, then the assumption\n431\t249\n432\t\n433\t######## PDF 261 / p.250\n434\tSUPERTRAINING\n435\tconcerning antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms \n436\tmust be questioned (Mellenbcrg & Khvan, 1982).\n437\tOne should consider the fact that OBLA (lactate threshold) and V02 max levels can change independently o \n438\teach other. It is interesting to note that, while there is some decrease in V(>2 max during the competition period \n439\tOBLA can rise (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Sirenko, 1979; Nurmckivan, 1974). Research indicates that blood lactate \n440\tconcentration can increase by some 6% as a result of training, while the VO2 max level increases by only 7% \n441\t(Williams et al, 1967). It has also been shown, that the mean change in VO2 max of qualified skaters is 5-10% in \n442\tthe competition period, while local blood flow is increased much more significantly (50-250%) (Mellenbcrg, \n443\t1981). This also corroborates the finding that effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in \n444\tenhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport, which thereby can meet the oxygen requirements of \n445\tthe tissues by decreasing reliance on the contribution of anacrobic metabolism.\n446\tConsequently, together with the rise in strength and oxidative qualities of the muscles, the redistribution of \n447\tbloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions fo r developing local muscle endurance. \n448\tSports exercises are performed by specific muscle groups and if metabolic processes in these muscles are \n449\tespecially intense, then the products of anacrobic metabolism accumulate, resulting in fatigue and decreased worii \n450\tcapacity. Therefore, adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature. For example, if one \n451\ttrains different muscle groups, then it is possible to achieve approximately equivalent functional effect at the level \n452\tof the cardiovascular-respiratory systems. However, it is displayed only in specific situations, i.e. while using \n453\tthose same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972). With skiers, running and imitation \n454\tskiing without poles raises the general functional level during the preparatory period, including VO2 max, but does \n455\tnot develop the specific physical preparedness necessary for speed of movement on skis. On the other hand, \n456\timitation skiing and indoor skiing on rollers with poles enhances local muscular endurance and vesicular reactions \n457\tin the muscles of the upper extremities, which leads to the efficient redistribution of blood flow and an increase in \n458\tspecific work-capacity for skiing (Yevstratov et al, 1975).\n459\tLocal muscular endurance is the athlete’s ability to produce the strength component o f movement fora \n460\tprolonged period without significant fatigue. For example, this may be seen in 400m sprinting, especially \n461\tregarding the changes in stride length and tempo with increase in sport mastery (Fig 4.23). It is clear that stride \n462\tlength is maintained with some decrease in tempo at the high-mastery level by means of local strength-endurance. \n463\tIn the fatigued state, athletes of different sex and level of qualification all display a greater decrease in stride length \n464\tthan tempo, but the decrease in speed is associated exclusively with the strength factor.\n465\tFigure 4.23 Change in stride length accompanying increase in the speed o f running 400m (Popov, 1972).\n466\tThus, the importance of local muscle endurance in sports requiring prolonged maintenance o f a specific work \n467\toutput, is obvious. However, practical ways of developing local muscular endurance still need serious research. \n468\tClearly, one should find ways to intensify muscular work in those regimes characterising the given sport and \n469\tutilise special strength exercises for this purpose (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\n470\tStrength Training and General Endurance\n471\tWith respect to what has just been discussed, one should examine the research showing that strength training leads \n472\tto a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, \n473\t1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of\n474\t250\n475\t\n476\t######## PDF 262 / p.251\n477\tSUPERTRAINING\n478\ttraining, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & \n479\tFilin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981). Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local \n480\t(strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport (Reindel, 1962; Fetz, 1965; Nett, 1964; Asmussen, 1969; Cook & \n481\tBymston, 1973; Simri, 1974; Nurmekivi, 1974), but in our time, it has not been given serious attention. Methods \n482\tof endurance development were aimed chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by \n483\tdistance training), which are a major factor in determining progress in cyclic sports, especially in longer distance \n484\tninning.\n485\tFurthermore, the above data justify the importance of solving the problem of enhancing endurance, where it \n486\tis considered to be a complex resultant of motor abilities (Reindel, 1962; Roskamm et al, 1952; Brogli & Antonov, \n487\t1969; Larson & Yocom, 1952; Posker & Steblo, 1967; Wilt, 1970). In accordance with this, general endurance is \n488\tdefined in terms of the cardiovascular-respiratory functions which provide the necessary oxygen and the \n489\tappropriate state of the neuromuscular system. From this standpoint, the development o f endurance should be \n490\tearned out in the form of a scheme of training complexes based on the interaction between the cardiovascular- \n491\trespiratory and motor systems.\n492\tThese characteristics of endurance agree with the concepts of the motor-visccral reflexes (Mogendovich, \n493\t1962) and the energetics of muscle contraction (Arshavsky, 1967). \n494\tIn accordance with these concepts, the \n495\tefficiency of muscular work is determined by the interdependence of motor activity, topography, sequentiality \n496\tand the work regime of the muscles involved, and the respiratory, circulatory and metabolic processes in the \n497\tbody.\n498\tWith respect to the interdependence of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor processes, the latter plays \n499\tthe key role insofar as functioning of the motor apparatus determines the training of the cardiovascular- \n500\trespiratory systems. It should be noted that the cooperation between the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor \n501\tsystems is important for improving work-capacity, not only in endurance sports, but in all sports. Therefore, it is \n502\ti serious error to draw a line between them, as is quite common in the methodology of special physical training.\n503\tThe Process of Functional Specialisation\n504\tIf we focus on the process of functional specialisation over many years of training, its most general features may \n505\tbe represented in an ascending flowchart (Fig 4.24). We are endowed with an enormous ability to execute any \n506\tmotor task and perfect this ability. We have flexible mechanisms of coordination, regulation and energy \n507\tproduction for motor function, and the ability to produce movements which require the display of speed, \n508\tstrength, or endurance. These mechanisms ensure the success of human activities in daily life and offer extensive \n509\tpossibilities for adaptation (Level 1). Changes in environmental conditions imposed by sport activities in training \n510\tand competition stimulate the motor function, which coordinates all muscular activity (Level 2). The motor \n511\tfunction specialises chiefly by developing the ability to display explosive force and specific endurance \n512\tindependent of the type of sport (Level 3).\n513\tFigure 4.24 The general sequence of the body's functional specialisation over many years of training (Siff & Verkhoshansky. 1999).\n514\tIt should be noted that specific endurance may be predominantly cardiovascular or local muscular in nature. In \n515\taddition, explosive force relates closely to the motor qualities of starting strength, accelcration-strength and \n516\tabsolute strength (see 3.2.1). The intensive stage of functional specialisation follows which, along with further \n517\timproving its survival capability, forms the structure of special physical preparedness (Level 4). The latter\n518\t251\n519\t\n520\t######## PDF 263 / p.252\n521\tSUPERTRAINING\n522\tdisplays its own form with respect to interaction of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems, which \n523\tsecure the athlete's high work-capacity.\n524\tWithin the confines of such structures, the functional systems interact in cooperative and subordinate \n525\trelationships. In the first case (cooperation) their relationship is characterised by the effective interaction of all the \n526\tsystems responsible for producing the working capabilities of the body, whereas in the second case \n527\t(subordination) the productivity o f one system is enhanced by the functional potentials of the others. The \n528\tstructure o f special physical preparedness is determined by the specific training regime, and does not relate to \n529\tconditions imposed by other sporting activities. This fact should be noted by anyone who may be contemplating \n530\tthe use of generalised cross training with various sports.\n531\tFactor Analysis\n532\tMethodical study of the structure of special physical preparedness may be achieved via statistical analysis of \n533\tthe characteristics which describe the various aspects of the athlete's work-capacity. Results of factor analysis \n534\tare especially informative in those cases where one o f the composite schemes describes the preparedness of \n535\tathletes of different qualification or the same athlete at different stages of training.\n536\tThis type of information, gathered from results in many sports, indicates that there are distinct changes in \n537\tthe structure o f an athlete's physical preparedness with increasing sports proficiency, with marked differences \n538\tbeing observed between athletes of high and low qualification.\n539\tThe data in Table 4.1 show that athletes (high jumpers. Class 1-MS), concurrently with increase in \n540\tproficiency, develop the specific ability to generate powerful force during the take-off in jumping exercises (factor \n541\tI) and perfect the ability to powerfully extend the body with the dynamic participation of the knee and back \n542\textensor muscles (factor III).\n543\tThe ability of the extensor muscles of the take-off leg to display explosive force under isometric conditions \n544\t(factor II) assumes major significance (see more details on explosive isometrics in 4.2.4). This ability serves as the \n545\tfoundation of factors IV and V which is characteristic of the structure of Class 2 and 3 athletes. At the same time, \n546\tindicators which characterise the explosive capabilities of the plantar flexors shift from factor II (Class 2 and 3 \n547\tathletes) to factor IV (Class 1 athletes and MS), the contribution of which diminishes significantly. On the whole, \n548\tthe role o f special physical preparedness of jumpers increases with level of qualification.\n549\tTabic 4.1 The factor structure (in %) o f Special Physical Preparedness of high jumpers, in terms o f the major factors displayed by a \n550\tlarge sample of competitors of different levels of qualification (Nikitin, 1983)\n551\tFactor Order\n552\tClass 2-3 A thletes\n553\tClass 1 - MS\n554\t1\n555\tGeneral athletic preparedness \n556\t29.6\n557\tSpecific athletic preparedness\n558\t27.4\n559\t11\n560\tPower of plantarflexors \n561\t19.6\n562\tPower of knee extension Isomet ncally\n563\t23.4\n564\tIII\n565\tPower of knee extension In dynamic regime \n566\t13.0\n567\tPower of knee extension dynamically\n568\t18.5\n569\tIV\n570\tIsometric strength of knee extensors \n571\t10.5\n572\tPower of plantarflexors\n573\t11.8\n574\tV\n575\tExplosive-strength of knee extensors (isometric) 6.5\n576\tAs an important practical consequcncc emanating from the data in Table 4.1, one needs to note that the \n577\tcontribution of isometric strength in the formation of the specific ability to generate explosive force in the take-off \n578\tassumes great significance for highly qualified athletes. This is due to the large loading of the muscles during the \n579\ttake-off, where the support (take-off) leg acts as a lever, and converts into vertical lift the angular velocity \n580\tacquired during the run-up. Furthermore, the plantarflexors play a diminishing role, because under intense \n581\toverloading the muscles of the support leg are already unable to make a significant contribution to the dynamics of \n582\tthe take-off.\n583\tThe nature of the changes in an athlete's structure of special fitness in cyclic sports is presented in Table 4.2. \n584\tOne should note the sharp rise in the role of energetic efficiency of special work and multifaceted technical \n585\tpreparedness, factors which make an increasing contribution to general development. One should also point out \n586\tthat the first two factors (power and aerobic capacity) of Class 1 athletes may be combined as one factor (i.e.\n587\t252\n588\t\n589\t######## PDF 264 / p.253\n590\tSUPERTRAINING\n591\taerobic efficiency) whose role at Master of Sport level decreases in the general structure of special fitness. At the \n592\tsame time the significance of anaerobic power increases slightly (factor 111).\n593\tTabic 4.2 The factor structure (in %) o f special fitness of skaters in terms o f the major factors displayed by a large sample of \n594\tcompetitors of different levels of qualification (Shchirkovyets & Rosovtsyev, 1977).\n595\tFactor Order\n596\tC lass 1 A thletes\n597\tM aster of Sport\n598\tI\n599\tAerobic Power \n600\t35.9\n601\tEnergetic efficiency of executing specific work 36.5\n602\tD\n603\tAerobic Capacity \n604\t23.3\n605\tMulti-faceted technical preparedness\n606\t21.9\n607\tIII\n608\tPower of anaerobic processes\n609\t16.8\n610\tPower of anaerobic processes\n611\t19.8\n612\tIV\n613\tEnergetic efficiency of executing specific work \n614\t2.7\n615\tAerobic efficiency \n616\t10.9\n617\tV\n618\tMulti-faceted technical preparedness\n619\t8.9\n620\tThese results indicate that a high level of aerobic potential still does not ensure top-level performance in skating. \n621\tTo efficiently generate a high level of aerobic power, it necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high \n622\tlevel of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity, thereby expanding the athlete’s motor capabilities.\n623\tThus, factor research offers a useful statistical model of the structure of the athlete's special physical \n624\tpreparedness (SPP). This structure can have different forms which are determined by the motor qualities \n625\tcharacteristic of the individual and the organisation of training. Therefore, when programming training it is \n626\tnecessary to take into consideration the general trends of change in the factor structure of special physical \n627\tpreparedness with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete and all \n628\tchanges in the level of and the interrelation between the main functional characteristics.\n629\t253\n630\t\n631\t######## PDF 265 / p.254\n632\t254\n633\t"}]},"uuid":"c2e5da2d-ce14-4ac8-b59e-50b9bb4643c7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:39:15.686Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/src.txt","content":"\n######## PDF 252 / p.241\nSUPERTRAINING\nThere is an ancient injunction that the weavers of Eastern carpet must include at least one flaw in their \nwork to recognisc that perfection is reserved for the Divine and not for humans. The above findings imply that \nijnilar asymmetry or lack of perfection may be desirable for all human physical performance and training.\n4.2.9 The Concept o f M uscle Isolation\nGenerations of muscle magazine authors have extolled the virtues of special ways of training to isolate specific \nmuscle groups. They have advocated certain machines, ways of fixating the joints, precise placement of the limbs \nud numerous other ingenious manoeuvres to ensure that one muscle is maximally recruited while others remain \nlargely dormant.\nEMG studies reveal that isolation of minute muscle groups is indeed possible under low intensities of \nloading (e.g. Basmajian, 1978). Intuitively, we are aware that low intensity actions such as eye-blinking, piano- \nplaying or relaxed speaking are due to the precise action of specific muscle groups, a fact which the EMG clearly \ncorroborates.\nHowever, if large loads are imposed on a given muscle group by intense resistance, isolation becomcs virtu- \nilly impossible. Immediately the load is applied, stabilising muscles become involved to ensure that the body or \nspecific joints remain stable, while the prime movers attempt to cope with the load. If the nervous system signals \nthat the prime movers are inadequate to execute the desired joint action, assistant movers, shunt muscles, \nemergency muscles and neutralises may all be recruited to augment the action of the prime movers.\nPhysiotherapists relying on PNF techniques sometimes impose maximal loading on a joint to elicit \ncompensatory or overflow activity o f muscles other than the ‘prime movers.* \nThis is not possible if the \nresistance imposed is inadequate to overload the ‘prime movers.* Similarly, the weight trainer who wishes to \nincrease hypertrophy or strength of a given muscle selects a weight which forces it to operate at high intensity. \nHie higher the intensity or the greater the duration of the exercise, the less likely is isolation of the prime mover, \neven if certain joints arc restrained as on an isokinetic test machine.\nIn many cases the tension developed in the stabilisers or assistant stabilisers will equal or exceed that of the \npnme movers. For example, during heavy standing tricep press-downs with cable machines, the abdominal \nmuscles, latissimus dorsi and erector spinae of the back may be as strongly contracted as the triceps, even though \nthis is supposedly an isolation exercise for the triceps. Analysis of many seated or reclining isolation exercises on \nmachines reveals that isolation simply docs not occur with heavy loads.\nThis is not to negate the value of so-called isolation exercises. Not only can they be useful during the early \nstages of injury rehabilitation, but overflow to other muscle groups may be very desirable (if the exercise is \nperformed safely), sincc it simultaneously offers isometric and dynamic training to many other muscle groups. It \ntsnecessary, however, to clarify the popular misconceptions surrounding the misleading concept of muscle \nisolation still proliferated by numerous instructors and in many training texts.\n4.3 Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of S trength \nTraining\nDevising methods of special strength training is associated firstly with the selection of the means and methods for \ndeveloping muscular strength. \nAH sporting movement is specific and goal-directed. Therefore, the strength \ndisplayed in the execution of each movement is also specific and goal-directed. One should not refer to strength in \ngeneral, but only in the context of the given task. In other words, the means and methods of strength training \nshould provide an adequate conditioning regime for the motor system in the special exercises and thereby ensure \ncontinued improvement in sporting performance.\nThe foundation of this assertion lies in the principle o f dynamic correspondence, which emphasizes that the \nmeans and methods of strength training for specific sports should be chosen to enhance the required motor \nqualities in terms o f :\n• The amplitude and direction of movement\n• The accentuated region of force production\n• The dynamics o f the effort\n• The rate and time of maximum force production\n• The regime of muscular work\n241\n\n######## PDF 253 / p.242\nSUPERTRAINING\n4.3.1 The Amplitude and D irection of M ovement\nThe criterion of correspondence with respect to the amplitude and direction originates from the patterns of the \nmovement relative to the adjacent body part. It determines which muscles are involved in the work and allows for \nanatomical peculiarities and the external conditions of work. Thus, the movement of the shoulder girdle of a row« \nor shot putter is about the same in amplitude, but the direction of the working force is different (Fig 4.17). In the \nfirst case the pulling of the oars involves shoulder extension, whereas in the second case it involves shoulder \nflexion acting in opposition to the force of inertia of the shot.\nThe importance of the correspondence of muscular work, in terms of the relevant criteria may be illustrated \nby the following example. In track-and-field training, jumpers and sprinters sometimes do exercises to develop the \nhip flexor muscles in a standing position on a hip flexor machine which offers resistance by means of a pad \npressing against the front of the thigh.\nHowever, the amplitude of thigh movement in running and jumping (when moving the legs forward) ij \nsignificantly greater than the amplitude of this exercise, and begins at an angle of about 210° in the hip joint, \nrelative to the torso. Therefore, the execution of this exercise in the standing position docs not duplicate the \nmcchanism of the movement in the sport specific exercise ( e.g. jumping or sprinting).\nFigure 4.17 Working amplitude of shoulder movement of a rower and a shotputter (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\nIf the athlete’s position changes (Fig 4.18), the criterion of the correspondence is met, not only with respect to the \namplitude of the movement, but also to the sporting movement, because the resistance imitates the inertial \nresistance of the mass of the leg rotating about the hip joint in the special exercise. By altering the load, the \nnumber of repetitions and the tempo of movement, the task of developing the strength to produce the required \nforce and strength-endurance, will be solved. Thus, to fulfill the criteria of correspondence with respect to the \namplitude and direction of movement, it is advisable to select the exact starting position and posture of the ath­\nlete, as well as to calculate the direction of action of the forces associated with the working links of the system \nand the additional load.\nThe line of action of the applied external resistance and of the loaded movement as a whole must also be \ntaken into account. For example, in middle-distancc running, skiing and skating, a knapsack full o f sand ora \nweight belt are sometimes used as resistance. However, the muscles which bear the load are those which resist the \nweight o f the body. This can increase the ability to cope with vertical loading and develop general strength- \nendurance, but docs not strengthen those muscles which propel the body horizontally.\nFigure 4.18 Exercise for developing hip flexion strength based on consideration for the accentuated part o f the working amplitude and \nthe resistance to the force of muscle contraction (SiflF & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\nSimilarly, a skater may exccute jumps on one leg on the floor or from a bench. These exercises strengthen the leg \nmuscles supporting the body and the static-endurance of the back muscles, but do not fully imitate the working of \nthe muscles for the push-off, where the force is directed backward.\n242\n\n######## PDF 254 / p.243\nSUPERTRAINING\nSkaters should use another method of resisted movement by changing the direction in which the force of \nresistance is acting (Fig 4.19). These methods to a large extent match the training exercise to the dynamics of the \nsport specific actions.\nFigure 4.19 Training means for increasing resistance during skating (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\nIn sport, the working force is developed by the simultaneous coordinated tension of the muscle groups which \nmove the various segments of the body. The most characteristic example of such cooperation of the working \nmuscles is the simultaneous flexion and extension at the two hip joints (e.g. in running, jumping, fencing, figure \nskating, basketball or tennis), where the angular movement o f one leg enhances the push-off movement of the \nother. It is useful to imitate this combination with special strength exercises which take into account the working \namplitude of the movement and the direction of the opposing force (as in Fig 4.20). On the other hand, it is \nusually inappropriate to perform the actual sporting movements with significantly large added loads in an attempt \nto provide exact simulation, since the added loading invariably alters the centres of gravity and rotation of the \nlimbs and body, thereby altering the underlying motor patterns and dispositions of joints relative to one another.\nFigure 4.20 Complex exercise for simultaneous strengthening of the hip flexors and extensors, and knee extensors (Verkhoshansky, \n1977).\nParachute R esistance T raining\nSometimes, small parachutes of different size (to offer different degrees of resistance) may be attached with a \nquick-releasc harness to the trunk of the athlete to offer a low-inertia form of resistance (Popov, 1966). Although \nthere have been claims that these parachutes were developed secretly in Moscow in 1988 (Brunner & Tabachnik \nSoviet Training and Recovery Methods 1990), this reference shows that Popov and others were researching their \nuse more than 20 years earlier.\nThe quick release may be used to decrease the resistance rapidly while the athlete is running and thereby offer \nthe benefits of contrast training, such as acceleration strength (see 7.1). Due largely to the efforts of emigre \nRussians such as Dr Ben Tabachnik, a former soviet track coach now living in the USA, these running parachutes \nhave become a popular training aid in the West, where they generally have been applied fairly randomly with little \nknowledge of pcriodising them optimally into overall training programmes. Appropriateness of use depends on \nan understanding of which strength qualities are required by the athlete at a given stage of training.\nIn particular, parachute training plays no role in the development of starting strength, which is produced \nunder isometric conditions. They, however, offer a safer alternative to the use of weights added to the extremities\n243\n\n######## PDF 255 / p.244\nSUPERTRAINING\n244\nor body, or dragged behind the athlete (e.g. a car tyre or sled), because they do not increase the vertical reaction \nforces acting on the body. The fact that the parachutes offer aerodynamic resistance which increases rapidly with \nvelocity (actually the square of the velocity) allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably. Thus, smaller \nparachutes are more suited for speed development, medium parachutes for speed-endurance and large parachutes \nfor specd-strcngth and acceleration-strength. Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance, so \nthat optimal development of these fitness or motor qualities depends on attempts to run at maximum speed for as \nlong as possible and implementation of various fartlck or interval methods. The drag force characteristically varies \nbetween 5 - 200 Newtons for running speeds of 6 -10 metres per second, depending on the size of the parachute \n(Zatsiorsky, 1995).\nQuick release of the parachute at maximum velocity can be helpful as a special contrast method in enhancing \nspeed and stride frequency. Training distances commonly vary between 30m and 150m, with smaller parachutes \nbeing used for the longer distances. If the basic principles of its application arc clearly understood, parachute \ntraining can be used successfully by distance runners, as well as sprint or distance cyclists and swimmers. As \nwith other resistance training, this method must be applied carefully so as not to disrupt motor skill and \ncoordination. In particular, it should be noted that parachutes offer the same degree o f resistance during both the \nsupport and non-support phases of running, thereby disrupting speed and acceleration of limb and body \nmovement during flight. \nIn addition, parachutes alter the relative position of the joints during landing, especially \nduring hurdling and jumping. \nAll of this can adversely affect several aspects of the degree o f dynamic \ncorrespondence of the training means to the actual sporting movements (as discussed throughout Ch 4.3).\nIn using parachutes, the principle of gradual progressive overloading should be applied, with larger parachutes \nbeing used over a period of weeks. In doing so, it is essential to periodically decrease the loading in any given \nworkout or microcycle and to apply the sudden release contrast method to enhance the training effect (see 1.16.3). \nIn any workout, it is common to progress rapidly after the warm-up to the use of large resistance parachutes, then \nconclude with a few repetitions using smaller parachutes.\nTo ensure that this aerodynamic resistance training does not leave after-effects which disturb one's normal \nneuromuscular skills, it is advisable to precede and follow all parachute sessions with the same exercises \nperformed without parachutes. During the preparatory weekly microcycles, parachute sessions typically are \nperformed two or three times a week on alternate days between other types of training session. They arc used \nmore frequently nearer the later stage of the preparatory phase immediately preceding the competitive season.\n4.3.2 The A ccentuated Region of Force Production\nMuscular effort changes during the course of every movement and maximum force is developed at the most \nappropriate instant for a given action. In ballistic movements this instant corresponds to the beginning part of the \nworking amplitude and in movements of mixed regimes of muscular work, this takes place at the instant of \nswitching from one regime to another (Verkhoshansky, 1977). Thus, the working amplitude always has an \naccentuated region where the maximum dynamic force occurs close to a specific joint angle. Based on this, the \ncriterion of correspondence includes the need to produce the required force at a specific joint angle.\nIn the previous example it should be noted that the accentuated region of the working amplitude of the leg \nmovement is near the beginning, as discussed earlier and as shown in the graph of hip flexion torque in the air (Fig \n4.21).\n\n######## PDF 256 / p.245\nSUPERTRAINING\nConsequently, the athletes referred to earlier who run with loads or use hip flexor machines regularly not \nonly do not reproduce the required full amplitude of the movement, but also lose the ability to train the muscles \nto generate the necessary force at the appropriate hip joint angle. This clcarly illustrates the importance of the \nstarting position for executing the special strength exercises that have a local effect on the motor system. The \nstrength exercise should not only reproduce the full amplitude of the movement but also the specific direction of \nresistance to the pull of the muscles.\nFollowing these requirements can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines \nwhich are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22). However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which \nfails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria. In example illustrated, the partner seated on the athlete's \nshoulders simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes, while the partner pulling the back leg \noffers resistance intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors. While this exercise may be appropriate if \napplied carefully, incorrect positioning or imposition of resistance can reduce its effectiveness markedly.\nFlgirt 4.22 A special strength exercise intended to simulate the flexion-extension action of the hip during sports activities, where the \nKccntuated regions o f force production can be incorrectly resisted and posture can be inappropriately held (Siff & Verkhoshansky, \n1999).\nIt is possible to develop force at the required joint angles with isometric exercises, because they can have a \nparticular value in this respect (see 4.2.4). With isometrics or quasi-isometrics over a given range of movement \none can locally influence certain muscle groups by paying particular attention to the accentuated regions of the \nmovements where they arc involved. Therefore, the selection of these exercises should be based on the joint angle \nit which maximal torque is developed in the special exercise.\n4.3.3 The Dynam ics of the Effort\nThe criterion of dynamic effort is the quantitative correspondence of the dynamics of the training means to the \nspecific sports movement. This criterion states that the intensity of the training stimulus should not be less than \nthat encountered in the given exercise at the corresponding level of sports proficiency and should even excced it. \nIn other words, the effort exerted in training should not be less than the effort generated in the specific sports \nmovement. One must now ask whether the maximum value or the mean value should be considered as the \ncriterion of correspondence of strength.\nDepending upon external conditions, a person can display maximal strength in two forms: in the movement \nofheavy loads or light loads. The maximum force can actually be larger in the sccond case than in the first, since \nthe lighter load can be rapidly accelerated (remembering that F = ma). However, it is unnecessary to exercise only \nwith light loads, even if the maximum achieved corresponds closely to the dynamics of the special exercise. It is \nimportant to consider first the duration and character of the effort.\nThe fact is that the external force displayed in these two cases is qualitatively different: in the first, \nmaximum force is determined by the absolute strength of the muscles, and in the sccond by the speed o f \ncontraction. Therefore, the strength and training effects of the respective movements are also different.\nFrom this it follows that, in striving to generate maximal effort in training, the athlete should become clearly \nacquainted with the type of strength which is needed and satisfy the criteria of correspondence according to the \nimount of effort and the movement time for the specific exercise. In other words, correspondence with respect to \nthe maximum and mean magnitude of strength can be determined only by calculating the speed of movement. If \nthe athlete finds the means for achieving this, he can be confident that he is on the right track in organising his \nspecial strength training.\n245\n\n######## PDF 257 / p.246\nSUPERTRAINING\n2 4 6\nThe following examples serve to clarify the issue. If the special exercise requires the athlete to overcome I \nlarge resistance at a relatively low speed, then training should be aimed primarily at developing maximal strength. \nConversely, if the athlete has to deal with a small resistance in the special exercise and execute the movement u \nmaximal speed, then, according to the criterion of correspondence, he should apply a moderate amount of effort \nand pay particular attention to the duration of the movement.\n4.3.4 The Rate and Tim e of Force Production\nThe criterion of correspondence regarding the rate of developing maximum force (see 2.2.1) complements the \ncriterion of dynamic effort and is particularly important in those sporting activities requiring explosive strength. \nWe have already stated that the strength displayed in training should be examined by taking into account its rate \nof production, which is equivalent to measuring the time taken to execute the movement. The production of \nexplosive strength implicates a brief-acting moderate force whose characteristics may be described in terms of \nfeatures such as the power and the impulse (F. Al) of the force, where s is the displacement and At is the brief \ntime interval over which the force F acts (sec 5.1). The following factors are relevant in this context:\nHere W refers to the work and may be defined in its integral form asW - jF(t).ds where s is the displacement \nproduced by the force F. Thus, if F is constant over the interval concerned, we obtain:\nThe right hand side of this equation quite simply reduces to the familiar basic expression for power \nPower \nP = F.V \n.....where V is the velocity of the object\nUnfortunately, this basic definition is applied without qualification in many strength training texts and can lead to I \nserious misunderstanding o f training theory and methods. If the force is not constant over the time period being I \nanalysed, then we have to rely on a more precise form such as equation (4.1) which reminds us to consider how \nthe force and velocity (as functions of time) vary over the time interval concerned. This can be done most \nconveniently by examining graphs of F versus V (e.g. Figs 3.15 - 3.18), F versus time and V versus time (e.g. Figs \n2.13,3.3, 3.4,3.14, 3.27,4.7).\nIt is especially important to be aware o f how the force-time (i.e. the RFD or Rate of Force Development) \ncurve changes with time (i.e., the slope of the F-t curve, shown in Fig 2.13). It is inadequate to refer to average or \npeak force alone. The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD I \nfactor (which was introduced in 2.2.1 as the factor that best defines explosive strength). This conclusion is vital \nto the design of strength training programmes to improve performance in any sport which involves explosive I \nactions. The emergence of plyometric training as an adjunct method of special strength training is one of the \npractical consequences of understanding the implications of the preceding analysis of power as a motor ability.\nIn all plyometric actions, the force changes very rapidly as a complicated transient spike function and cannot \nbe regarded as a constant throughout the rebound phase. In such cases it is better to rely on a force plate or high \nspeed video methods to analyse the movement than to attempt to apply elementary biomechanical calculations \nthat will inevitably produce grave errors.\nThe other factor used to analyse explosive strength, the Reactivity Coefficient, was defined earlier (2.2.1) as the \nRFD relative to one's bodyweight (or the weight of any object being moved rapidly):\nReactivity Coefficient \nRC = Fmax/ tmax.mg = RFDmax^W\nwhere Fmax is the maximum force exerted, tmax is the time taken to reach this maximum, the mass is m and g is the \nacceleration due to gravity = 9.8m/s2.\nThe above equations may be used to evaluate explosive strength, although the significance of each of them can \nbe different, depending on conditions. Thus, evaluation using calculations of the impulse of the force is acceptable \nonly when it is not necessary to compare different movements. If such a need arises, then the evaluation is valid \nonly if the time taken is identical for each movement, a situation that does not occur in human movement. \nTherefore, in this particular case, determination of the impulse of the force is irrelevant.\n\n######## PDF 258 / p.247\nSUPERTRAININC\nIn those cases where the working effort is displayed over a brief time interval, a necessary condition of the \nmovement’s effectiveness is the speed with which maximum effort is developed. In other words, if it is necessary \nfor the athlete to produce a large force within some time limit, he must do it quickly and use a high RFD. Such a \nnecessity is dictated by the conditions of the activity and by the biomechanical characteristics of the \nmusculoskeletal system, especially the working amplitude of the movement.\nThus, the RFD, or rate with which the required maximum force is developed (determined from the slope of \nthe force-time curve), is an important criterion of correspondence for resolving the task of special strength training \n(sec 2.2.1), which relates directly to the characteristics of the neuromuscular system. This again emphasizes the \nfact that all training is primarily neuromuscular and that training based entirely on variables such as exercise \nchoice, load, sets and repetitions is highly inadequate for producing sport specific fitness.\nIt should be stressed that fulfillment of the criterion of correspondence regarding strength and the speed with \nwhich it achieves a maximum creates its own unique problem, the successful resolution of which depends upon \nthe efficiency of the neuromuscular training process.\n4.3.5 The Regim e of Muscular Work\nThe criterion of correspondence regarding the sports regime relies on determining the character of the muscular \nwork involved. In particular, the regime of muscular work should be taken into consideration for selecting the \nmeans and methods of special strength training. \nThe fact is that, depending upon the character of its execution, \nthe same means can solve different tasks. For example, boxers and shot putters need to execute the same exercise \n(e.g. performing a thrusting arm movement against the resistance of a pulley machine) with different weights, \ntempos, number of sets and repetitions, because boxing requires primarily a quick, unresisted movement and the \nability to repeat it many times without significant decrease in force, whereas shotputting involves rapid single \nmovements against a specific resistance.\nThe selection of a regime of muscular work is difficult where a specific motor action is concerned. However, \nit is even more difficult in all-round sports like decathlon, gymnastics and modem pentathlon. Therefore, the \nproblem of selecting a regime of muscular work involves two obvious tasks:\n• selection of the regime for a specific motor action (e.g. the key elements in an exercise)\n• selection of the main regime which will best develop all of the diverse muscular activities in all-round training.\nThe latter issue is still far from being resolved, although the evolution of training methods in sport indicates that it \nshould be more thoroughly understood in the near future.\nAlmost one hundred years of experience in training all-round track athletes indicates that their fundamental \ntraining regime is speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type. This was corroborated by \nusing statistical analysis to identify the key exercises in the 'all-round' complex (Zatsiorsky, 1966; Lukauskas, \n1967; Mamdzhanyan, 1976). To corroborate this idea one should examine the preparation of gymnasts. For a long \ntime they adhered to primarily static training, with many of them including some dynamic exercises such as \njumping. The progress of competitive standards and the necessity to master complex elements such as jumping \nwith extensive longitudinal rotation compelled them to examine the value of the cyclic work regime and reconsider \nit in favour of dynamic muscular tension.\nThus, it may be concluded that cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably should be regarded as the most \nimportant in all-round training. It is also necessary to add other regimes, determined by the motor skills required \nby the specific sport. However, this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\nIn conjunction with perfecting motor skills in a specific activity, one should consider the importance of \nchanging from one activity to another in those exercises where this is necessary. Thus, an athlete's results in the \nlong jump are determined mainly by the ability to switch from the cyclic regime of muscular work in running, to \nexplosive effort in the take-off. For instance, good gymnasts are capable of switching rapidly from dynamic \nexplosive effort to isometric tension. The methodology underlying the development of these complex capabilities \nis the aim of technical training in all sport. Its resolution is the goal of special strength training.\n4.3.6 Correspondence of Training Means to the Sports M ovem ents\nThe scientific understanding of the character of muscular work and motor strength is derived from analysis of the \nathlete's movements, i.e. the entire complex of interacting motor actions which resolve the motor tasks most\n247\n\n######## PDF 259 / p.248\nSUPERTRAINING\neffectively. The most convenient way o f doing this is to use a high speed video system to record and analyse \neach movement at intervals separated by fractions of a second.\nThe kinematic structure characterises the interrelation of the separate movements in space and time and \nhelps to identify those actions which play either a fundamental or supportive role in determining motor skill. \nFrom here it is easy to take one further step to determine the means of perfecting the movements and especially \nthe means and methods of developing functional strength. Naturally, knowledge of the movement will be more \nprecise if it is based on quantitative information which can be obtained only from computerised video analysis.\nThe analysis of the kinematics of a motor action to model its mechanisms can be based on scrutiny of the \nphasic structure of the movement (according to Donskoi). The act as a whole or its elements can always be \ndivided into separate phases in which the direction of movement, the application of force and the character of \nmuscular work are distinguished. The interrelation between separate phases enables one to gain some idea of the \nmuscle actions and the movement as a whole, particularly during critical phases of the task. Such a representation \nwill be more complete if forces are simultaneously recorded along with the video images (e.g. using a force plate or \ncable tensiometer), thereby permitting the total interaction of the person with the external objects to be analysed.\nAs has already been stated, full reproduction of the complex interaction of the different types o f fitness \nstrength is not always possible in training. It becomes necessary to exercise locally the interacting muscle groups \nthat are crucial for controlling specific movements in a sporting task, which suggests that the training load be \nincreased. Such exercise should be based on the characteristics for fonning and developing the kincsiological \nstructure of the motor action (see 2.1.2, 2.1.3). It has been established that, in the course of mastering a motor \nact, the elements of the kinesiological structure develop in a particular pattern of heterochronicity, depending on \ntheir origin and manner of interdependence (see 2.2.3).\nThe development of some elements is determined by their direct interaction with external objects, whereas \nothers arc determined by indirect interaction with these objects, so that they develop at a slower rate. An \nindispensable condition for forming and developing the biodynamic structure is the relatively harmonious \ndevelopment of its elements. This task can be successfully undertaken only by means of an organised system of \nspecial strength training.\nThus, it must be emphasized again that success in selecting the means of special strength training is \ndetermined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements.\n4.4 Strength Training and General Endurance\nThe scientifically corroborated concepts conccming energy production for muscular work play an important role \nin solving the practical problems of training. Analysis of the functional specialisation of the body solely at the \nlevel of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate, especially if the physiology of endurance \nis considered with respect to the respiratory functions and maximum oxygen consumption (VO2 max) alone. Since \nexisting regimes of endurance training are still based on such ideas, improvement of modem methods of endurance \ndevelopment is now particularly urgent. This question is examined in detail below:\n1. Athletes who have equivalent V02 max levels show different results, and conversely, athletes with different \nlevels o f aerobic efficicncy demonstrate the same results. For example, top middle-distance runners of the \n1940s had the same VO2 max as today's athletes, yet there are major differences in performance (Faulkner, \n1968). There arc no statistically reliable differences between athletes of different qualification regarding the \ndevelopment of aerobic potential, although there are significant differences with respect to anaerobic effi­\nciency (Nabotnikova, 1972; Serafimova, 1974; Rusko, 1976).\n2. The VO2 max levels in highly-qualified athletes stabilises, but results continue to improve. For example, over \na 4-5 year period the V02 max levels of the best Soviet and Swedish skaters stabilised, but their results still \nimproved from year to year (Orlov & Sharova, 1977). V02 max levels remained practically unchanged in \nqualified road cyclists over many years of training, yet their performances improved (Melenberg et al, 1972).\n3. A significant dccrcasc in V02 max levels has been observed during the competition period, especially in \ncyclists (Bakhvalov, 1974; Zatsiorsky et al, 1974), swimmers (Scrafimov, 1974; Volkov ct al, 1974; \nNaumenko, 1978), skiers (Vasilicv & Trunin, 1974; Zima ct al, 1975), skaters (Stenin, 1973; Vavilov, 1977), \nmiddlc-distance runners (Loktyev, 1978, Borisov, 1979; Sirenko, 1981) and hockey players (Koloskov, \n1976). There is a decreasing correlation between V02 max and improving sport results. The correlation\n248\n\n######## PDF 260 / p.249\nSUPERTRAINING\ncoefficient is R = 0.7 during the preparation period, whereas it is 0.4 during the competitive period (Vasiliev \n& Trunin, 1974; Melenberg, 1981).\nThis data indicates that V02 max on its own is no guarantee of outstanding performance. It is now recognised \nthat, apart from aerobic power, other factors play a role, such as the ability to perform work for a prolonged \nperiod at a level of oxygen consumption close to the V02 max, and the ability to effectively utilise available \nenergy under competitive conditions. This concerns the decrease in V02 max during the competition period, a \nphenomenon possibly explained by the decreasing trainability of aerobic processes or by an inherent antagonism \nbetween aerobic and anaerobic processes (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Volkov, 1975).\nOxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\nPractical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential \nto utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of \noxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated. Anaerobic work is \napproximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's \nadvantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include \nmaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950).\nSince OBLA depends on V02 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to \ncreate some reserve of aerobic power by the end o f the preparatory period to avoid a decrease in V02 max during \nthe competition period, which may lower the specific work-capacity. Considering that a significant amount of the \nenergy production of intense muscular work is achieved by recruiting the glycolytic mechanism of ATP \nresynthesis, it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration.\nHowever, recent research indicates that an increase in endurance is associated more with enhancing the \nability of the musclcs to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the \nimount of oxygen in the bloodstream and improving the oxygen supply to the working muscles (Ekblom, 1969; \nRowell, 1971; Sal tin, 1974). Consequently, it is not simply the magnitude of V02 max that determines endurance, \nbut intramuscular factors which facilitate adaptation of the muscles to prolonged intense work. A rise in \nmtramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the \nmuscles decrease the rate of glycolysis (Newshalme & Randle, 1964; Saltin & Karlsson, 1971; Paul et al, 1966; \nGnmby et al, 1967). At the same time, there is a significant drop in the end products of metabolism, including the \nspeed of oxidation of lactate in the working muscles (Jorfeldt, 1970). It is in the skeletal muscles and not in the \nliver and myocardium, as has always been believed, where there is a drop in lactate during and after work \n(Jorfeldt, 1970; Knutten, 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen, 1973).\nThus, the development o f endurance is associated with functional specialisation o f the skeletal muscles, \nparticularly the enhancement o f their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement o f \ncardiorespiratory ability. Consequently, the fundamental emphasis in developing endurance should be to decrease \nthe proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise \nlactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate. In other \nwords, in addition to utilising the aerobic pathway of energy production with maximum efficiency, the develop­\nment of endurance should be aimed chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities \nof the muscles, which is the main reason for a high lactate concentration. Recently, haemodynamic factors have \nbeen shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work \n(Vasiliev, 1970, 1974; Ozolin & Partsik, 1970; Danilov, 1980; Kurbanov, 1977; Rowell, 1971; Kaijser, 1970; \nforlsson, 1971; Gollnick et al, 1975). The redistribution o f blood and an increase in circulation to the working \nnuscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements o f the muscles and the removal o f anaerobic metabolites.\nDifferentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow and an optimal \nblood supply to the working muscles takes place during the competition period as a result of the extensive aerobic \nloading executed during the preparatory period. For example, research has shown that improvement in cycling \nperformance (in 25 km road racing) is accompanied by a decrease in V02 max during the competition period, while \npeak blood flow to the lower extremities increases. At the same time, cardiac output decreases during the \ncompetition period, a measure which reflects the efficiency of the cardiovascular system (Stepochkin et al, 1970). \nThe very same conditions which create economy of aerobic energy production decrease the involvement of \nglycolytic processes and, consequently, lessen dependence on V02 max. In this way, it is possible to eliminate \nthe basic cause of the decrease in VO2 max during the competition period. If this is so, then the assumption\n249\n\n######## PDF 261 / p.250\nSUPERTRAINING\nconcerning antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms \nmust be questioned (Mellenbcrg & Khvan, 1982).\nOne should consider the fact that OBLA (lactate threshold) and V02 max levels can change independently o \neach other. It is interesting to note that, while there is some decrease in V(>2 max during the competition period \nOBLA can rise (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Sirenko, 1979; Nurmckivan, 1974). Research indicates that blood lactate \nconcentration can increase by some 6% as a result of training, while the VO2 max level increases by only 7% \n(Williams et al, 1967). It has also been shown, that the mean change in VO2 max of qualified skaters is 5-10% in \nthe competition period, while local blood flow is increased much more significantly (50-250%) (Mellenbcrg, \n1981). This also corroborates the finding that effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in \nenhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport, which thereby can meet the oxygen requirements of \nthe tissues by decreasing reliance on the contribution of anacrobic metabolism.\nConsequently, together with the rise in strength and oxidative qualities of the muscles, the redistribution of \nbloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions fo r developing local muscle endurance. \nSports exercises are performed by specific muscle groups and if metabolic processes in these muscles are \nespecially intense, then the products of anacrobic metabolism accumulate, resulting in fatigue and decreased worii \ncapacity. Therefore, adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature. For example, if one \ntrains different muscle groups, then it is possible to achieve approximately equivalent functional effect at the level \nof the cardiovascular-respiratory systems. However, it is displayed only in specific situations, i.e. while using \nthose same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972). With skiers, running and imitation \nskiing without poles raises the general functional level during the preparatory period, including VO2 max, but does \nnot develop the specific physical preparedness necessary for speed of movement on skis. On the other hand, \nimitation skiing and indoor skiing on rollers with poles enhances local muscular endurance and vesicular reactions \nin the muscles of the upper extremities, which leads to the efficient redistribution of blood flow and an increase in \nspecific work-capacity for skiing (Yevstratov et al, 1975).\nLocal muscular endurance is the athlete’s ability to produce the strength component o f movement fora \nprolonged period without significant fatigue. For example, this may be seen in 400m sprinting, especially \nregarding the changes in stride length and tempo with increase in sport mastery (Fig 4.23). It is clear that stride \nlength is maintained with some decrease in tempo at the high-mastery level by means of local strength-endurance. \nIn the fatigued state, athletes of different sex and level of qualification all display a greater decrease in stride length \nthan tempo, but the decrease in speed is associated exclusively with the strength factor.\nFigure 4.23 Change in stride length accompanying increase in the speed o f running 400m (Popov, 1972).\nThus, the importance of local muscle endurance in sports requiring prolonged maintenance o f a specific work \noutput, is obvious. However, practical ways of developing local muscular endurance still need serious research. \nClearly, one should find ways to intensify muscular work in those regimes characterising the given sport and \nutilise special strength exercises for this purpose (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\nStrength Training and General Endurance\nWith respect to what has just been discussed, one should examine the research showing that strength training leads \nto a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, \n1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of\n250\n\n######## PDF 262 / p.251\nSUPERTRAINING\ntraining, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & \nFilin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981). Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local \n(strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport (Reindel, 1962; Fetz, 1965; Nett, 1964; Asmussen, 1969; Cook & \nBymston, 1973; Simri, 1974; Nurmekivi, 1974), but in our time, it has not been given serious attention. Methods \nof endurance development were aimed chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by \ndistance training), which are a major factor in determining progress in cyclic sports, especially in longer distance \nninning.\nFurthermore, the above data justify the importance of solving the problem of enhancing endurance, where it \nis considered to be a complex resultant of motor abilities (Reindel, 1962; Roskamm et al, 1952; Brogli & Antonov, \n1969; Larson & Yocom, 1952; Posker & Steblo, 1967; Wilt, 1970). In accordance with this, general endurance is \ndefined in terms of the cardiovascular-respiratory functions which provide the necessary oxygen and the \nappropriate state of the neuromuscular system. From this standpoint, the development o f endurance should be \nearned out in the form of a scheme of training complexes based on the interaction between the cardiovascular- \nrespiratory and motor systems.\nThese characteristics of endurance agree with the concepts of the motor-visccral reflexes (Mogendovich, \n1962) and the energetics of muscle contraction (Arshavsky, 1967). \nIn accordance with these concepts, the \nefficiency of muscular work is determined by the interdependence of motor activity, topography, sequentiality \nand the work regime of the muscles involved, and the respiratory, circulatory and metabolic processes in the \nbody.\nWith respect to the interdependence of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor processes, the latter plays \nthe key role insofar as functioning of the motor apparatus determines the training of the cardiovascular- \nrespiratory systems. It should be noted that the cooperation between the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor \nsystems is important for improving work-capacity, not only in endurance sports, but in all sports. Therefore, it is \ni serious error to draw a line between them, as is quite common in the methodology of special physical training.\nThe Process of Functional Specialisation\nIf we focus on the process of functional specialisation over many years of training, its most general features may \nbe represented in an ascending flowchart (Fig 4.24). We are endowed with an enormous ability to execute any \nmotor task and perfect this ability. We have flexible mechanisms of coordination, regulation and energy \nproduction for motor function, and the ability to produce movements which require the display of speed, \nstrength, or endurance. These mechanisms ensure the success of human activities in daily life and offer extensive \npossibilities for adaptation (Level 1). Changes in environmental conditions imposed by sport activities in training \nand competition stimulate the motor function, which coordinates all muscular activity (Level 2). The motor \nfunction specialises chiefly by developing the ability to display explosive force and specific endurance \nindependent of the type of sport (Level 3).\nFigure 4.24 The general sequence of the body's functional specialisation over many years of training (Siff & Verkhoshansky. 1999).\nIt should be noted that specific endurance may be predominantly cardiovascular or local muscular in nature. In \naddition, explosive force relates closely to the motor qualities of starting strength, accelcration-strength and \nabsolute strength (see 3.2.1). The intensive stage of functional specialisation follows which, along with further \nimproving its survival capability, forms the structure of special physical preparedness (Level 4). The latter\n251\n\n######## PDF 263 / p.252\nSUPERTRAINING\ndisplays its own form with respect to interaction of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems, which \nsecure the athlete's high work-capacity.\nWithin the confines of such structures, the functional systems interact in cooperative and subordinate \nrelationships. In the first case (cooperation) their relationship is characterised by the effective interaction of all the \nsystems responsible for producing the working capabilities of the body, whereas in the second case \n(subordination) the productivity o f one system is enhanced by the functional potentials of the others. The \nstructure o f special physical preparedness is determined by the specific training regime, and does not relate to \nconditions imposed by other sporting activities. This fact should be noted by anyone who may be contemplating \nthe use of generalised cross training with various sports.\nFactor Analysis\nMethodical study of the structure of special physical preparedness may be achieved via statistical analysis of \nthe characteristics which describe the various aspects of the athlete's work-capacity. Results of factor analysis \nare especially informative in those cases where one o f the composite schemes describes the preparedness of \nathletes of different qualification or the same athlete at different stages of training.\nThis type of information, gathered from results in many sports, indicates that there are distinct changes in \nthe structure o f an athlete's physical preparedness with increasing sports proficiency, with marked differences \nbeing observed between athletes of high and low qualification.\nThe data in Table 4.1 show that athletes (high jumpers. Class 1-MS), concurrently with increase in \nproficiency, develop the specific ability to generate powerful force during the take-off in jumping exercises (factor \nI) and perfect the ability to powerfully extend the body with the dynamic participation of the knee and back \nextensor muscles (factor III).\nThe ability of the extensor muscles of the take-off leg to display explosive force under isometric conditions \n(factor II) assumes major significance (see more details on explosive isometrics in 4.2.4). This ability serves as the \nfoundation of factors IV and V which is characteristic of the structure of Class 2 and 3 athletes. At the same time, \nindicators which characterise the explosive capabilities of the plantar flexors shift from factor II (Class 2 and 3 \nathletes) to factor IV (Class 1 athletes and MS), the contribution of which diminishes significantly. On the whole, \nthe role o f special physical preparedness of jumpers increases with level of qualification.\nTabic 4.1 The factor structure (in %) o f Special Physical Preparedness of high jumpers, in terms o f the major factors displayed by a \nlarge sample of competitors of different levels of qualification (Nikitin, 1983)\nFactor Order\nClass 2-3 A thletes\nClass 1 - MS\n1\nGeneral athletic preparedness \n29.6\nSpecific athletic preparedness\n27.4\n11\nPower of plantarflexors \n19.6\nPower of knee extension Isomet ncally\n23.4\nIII\nPower of knee extension In dynamic regime \n13.0\nPower of knee extension dynamically\n18.5\nIV\nIsometric strength of knee extensors \n10.5\nPower of plantarflexors\n11.8\nV\nExplosive-strength of knee extensors (isometric) 6.5\nAs an important practical consequcncc emanating from the data in Table 4.1, one needs to note that the \ncontribution of isometric strength in the formation of the specific ability to generate explosive force in the take-off \nassumes great significance for highly qualified athletes. This is due to the large loading of the muscles during the \ntake-off, where the support (take-off) leg acts as a lever, and converts into vertical lift the angular velocity \nacquired during the run-up. Furthermore, the plantarflexors play a diminishing role, because under intense \noverloading the muscles of the support leg are already unable to make a significant contribution to the dynamics of \nthe take-off.\nThe nature of the changes in an athlete's structure of special fitness in cyclic sports is presented in Table 4.2. \nOne should note the sharp rise in the role of energetic efficiency of special work and multifaceted technical \npreparedness, factors which make an increasing contribution to general development. One should also point out \nthat the first two factors (power and aerobic capacity) of Class 1 athletes may be combined as one factor (i.e.\n252\n\n######## PDF 264 / p.253\nSUPERTRAINING\naerobic efficiency) whose role at Master of Sport level decreases in the general structure of special fitness. At the \nsame time the significance of anaerobic power increases slightly (factor 111).\nTabic 4.2 The factor structure (in %) o f special fitness of skaters in terms o f the major factors displayed by a large sample of \ncompetitors of different levels of qualification (Shchirkovyets & Rosovtsyev, 1977).\nFactor Order\nC lass 1 A thletes\nM aster of Sport\nI\nAerobic Power \n35.9\nEnergetic efficiency of executing specific work 36.5\nD\nAerobic Capacity \n23.3\nMulti-faceted technical preparedness\n21.9\nIII\nPower of anaerobic processes\n16.8\nPower of anaerobic processes\n19.8\nIV\nEnergetic efficiency of executing specific work \n2.7\nAerobic efficiency \n10.9\nV\nMulti-faceted technical preparedness\n8.9\nThese results indicate that a high level of aerobic potential still does not ensure top-level performance in skating. \nTo efficiently generate a high level of aerobic power, it necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high \nlevel of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity, thereby expanding the athlete’s motor capabilities.\nThus, factor research offers a useful statistical model of the structure of the athlete's special physical \npreparedness (SPP). This structure can have different forms which are determined by the motor qualities \ncharacteristic of the individual and the organisation of training. Therefore, when programming training it is \nnecessary to take into consideration the general trends of change in the factor structure of special physical \npreparedness with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete and all \nchanges in the level of and the interrelation between the main functional characteristics.\n253\n\n######## PDF 265 / p.254\n254\n","numLines":633,"startLine":1,"totalLines":633}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45168860-27b1-4ba5-b3de-30fc3d5649b3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
