{"parentUuid":"239e02ec-906d-4c6a-97fc-3fb86ae27565","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffUdP3Dof7pg6YF2oMky","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XCT19cUNFzv7oBKPLUfkqG","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e2.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3193,"cache_read_input_tokens":126868,"output_tokens":113,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3193},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":113,"cache_read_input_tokens":126868,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3193,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3193,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XCT19cUNFzv7oBKPLUfkqG":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e2.txt"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffUdFJtD7Nn5H7P13GrD","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d417d493-e991-448b-b7ae-3a9c5a3d7cc2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:40:19.736Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d417d493-e991-448b-b7ae-3a9c5a3d7cc2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XCT19cUNFzv7oBKPLUfkqG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=================== heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md\n2\t---\n3\ttype: library-entry\n4\tkind: heuristic\n5\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n6\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n7\tchapter: \"4.3.4\"\n8\ttopic: rate and time of force production criterion\n9\tcategory: [rate-of-force-development, explosive-strength, specificity, testing]\n10\tconfidence: supported\n11\tingest-tier: full-text\n12\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t# Use the Rate of Force Development as a Correspondence Criterion; Do Not Use Impulse to Compare Different Movements\n16\t\n17\t**What it is** — The criterion of correspondence for \"the rate of developing maximum force\" complements the dynamic-effort criterion and \"is particularly important in those sporting activities requiring explosive strength\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). \"The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD factor,\" which the authors call \"vital\" to programme design for any sport with explosive actions, and they name plyometric training as \"one of the practical consequences\" of this analysis. Explosive strength may be evaluated by power, impulse (F.Δt) or the Reactivity Coefficient (RC = Fmax/tmax.mg = RFDmax/W), \"although the significance of each of them can be different\": impulse \"is acceptable only when it is not necessary to compare different movements\"; comparing movements would be valid \"only if the time taken is identical for each movement, a situation that does not occur in human movement,\" so for that purpose \"determination of the impulse of the force is irrelevant\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Where effort lasts only briefly, \"if it is necessary for the athlete to produce a large force within some time limit, he must do it quickly and use a high RFD,\" a necessity set by the activity and \"especially the working amplitude of the movement.\" RFD \"is an important criterion of correspondence ... which relates directly to the characteristics of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"all training is primarily neuromuscular and ... training based entirely on variables such as exercise choice, load, sets and repetitions is highly inadequate for producing sport specific fitness\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n18\t\n19\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: when the time available is limited by the movement's amplitude, how fast force rises decides how much force can be applied; and impulse depends on duration, so two movements of different duration cannot be compared on it. They tie RFD to \"the characteristics of the neuromuscular system\" without further mechanism here.\n20\t\n21\t**When to apply** — When an action has a short force-application window (jumps, sprints, throws, blocking contact): pick exercises and tests that reproduce the rate of force rise, not just peak force; use RFD or RC rather than impulse when comparing different exercises or tests. Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing is the daily readiness marker [**Explicit**, `docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74], and the CMJ family is run on ForceDecks [**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**, line 77]. Inference: mRSI is a time-normalised jump index, so it fits this criterion's preference for rate over impulse; the book does not discuss mRSI.\n22\t\n23\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors' rejection of impulse is scoped to comparing different movements; they accept it within one movement. Their statement that training built only on exercise choice, load, sets and repetitions is \"highly inadequate\" is a strong judgment without cited data. The RC formula repeats the 2.2.1 definition; the book defines RC in more than one way elsewhere (see [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]), so check which form a source means.\n24\t\n25\t**Example** — The book's own: plyometric training as the practical consequence of treating RFD as a trainable factor (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246).\n26\t\n27\t**Related** — [[claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--explosive-strength-deficit-and-rate-of-force-development-indices]].\n28\t=================== heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md\n29\t---\n30\ttype: library-entry\n31\tkind: heuristic\n32\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n33\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n34\tchapter: \"4.3.5\"\n35\ttopic: regime of muscular work criterion\n36\tcategory: [specificity, programming, load-selection, exercise-selection]\n37\tconfidence: supported\n38\tingest-tier: full-text\n39\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n40\t---\n41\t\n42\t# The Same Exercise Serves Different Sports Only If Its Regime Changes: Load, Tempo, Sets and Repetitions Must Match the Sport's Work\n43\t\n44\t**What it is** — The fifth correspondence criterion \"relies on determining the character of the muscular work involved,\" and the regime \"should be taken into consideration for selecting the means and methods of special strength training,\" because \"depending upon the character of its execution, the same means can solve different tasks.\" The authors' example: \"boxers and shot putters need to execute the same exercise (e.g. performing a thrusting arm movement against the resistance of a pulley machine) with different weights, tempos, number of sets and repetitions, because boxing requires primarily a quick, unresisted movement and the ability to repeat it many times without significant decrease in force, whereas shotputting involves rapid single movements against a specific resistance.\" The problem has two tasks: \"selection of the regime for a specific motor action (e.g. the key elements in an exercise)\" and \"selection of the main regime which will best develop all of the diverse muscular activities in all-round training\"; the second \"is still far from being resolved.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.)\n45\t\n46\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is that the training effect is set by how an exercise is executed, not by the exercise's name or shape, so the regime parameters must reproduce the sport's demand (repeated fast unresisted actions for the boxer, single fast actions against a set resistance for the shot putter). They give no further mechanism.\n47\t\n48\t**When to apply** — After an exercise passes the movement criteria (amplitude, direction, accentuated region), set its loading parameters from the sport's regime: repeated low-resistance fast actions call for light loads, fast tempo and many repetitions; single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery. Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of 'sport specific.'\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102). Inference: this criterion adds that position on that spectrum depends on how an exercise is loaded, not only which exercise it is.\n49\t\n50\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives the boxer and shot putter contrast without numbers, so it does not prescribe specific loads. For all-round sports (decathlon, gymnastics, modern pentathlon) the authors say the choice of a main regime is unresolved (see [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]). Regime matching does not replace the other four criteria.\n51\t\n52\t**Example** — The book's own: a pulley-machine thrusting arm movement done by a boxer (light, fast, many repetitions) and by a shot putter (heavier, single fast efforts) (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n53\t\n54\t**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]], [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]], [[heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination]] (4.2, choice among isometric, eccentric and other regimes), [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]] (Evans applies the regime criterion to a wrestling takedown).\n55\t=================== claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events.md\n56\t---\n57\ttype: library-entry\n58\tkind: claim\n59\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n60\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n61\tchapter: \"4.3.5\"\n62\ttopic: switching between regimes of work\n63\tcategory: [specificity, jumping, technique, special-strength]\n64\tconfidence: untested\n65\tingest-tier: full-text\n66\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t# In Mixed-Regime Events, the Ability to Switch Quickly from One Regime of Muscular Work to Another Decides the Result\n70\t\n71\t**What it is** — \"In conjunction with perfecting motor skills in a specific activity, one should consider the importance of changing from one activity to another in those exercises where this is necessary.\" The authors' examples: \"an athlete's results in the long jump are determined mainly by the ability to switch from the cyclic regime of muscular work in running, to explosive effort in the take-off,\" and \"good gymnasts are capable of switching rapidly from dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension.\" \"The methodology underlying the development of these complex capabilities is the aim of technical training in all sport. Its resolution is the goal of special strength training.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.) In 4.3.2 the authors also place the peak of force in mixed-regime movements \"at the instant of switching from one regime to another\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244).\n72\t\n73\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the long-jump \"determined mainly by\" statement. Inference: read with 4.3.2, the switch is where peak force falls, so a weak transition would cost force at the moment it matters most; this link is drawn here, not in the text.\n74\t\n75\t**When to apply** — For events that chain regimes (run-up into take-off, approach into a plant, a sprint into a cut, a dynamic move into a held position): include special strength work that reproduces the transition itself, not only each regime separately. Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence, including \"regime of muscular work,\" governs transfer. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: in football, a block or tackle that starts from a run and ends in a held isometric contest is a regime switch of the kind described; the book does not discuss football.\n76\t\n77\t**When NOT to apply** — The long-jump claim (\"determined mainly by\") has no cited study or data in the text. The authors assign the problem to both technical training and special strength training without saying how to split the work, so this is a direction, not a method.\n78\t\n79\t**Example** — The book's own: the long jumper switching from cyclic running to explosive take-off; the gymnast switching from dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n80\t\n81\t**Related** — [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]] (4.3.2, the switch as the force peak), [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]].\n82\t=================== claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md\n83\t---\n84\ttype: library-entry\n85\tkind: claim\n86\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n87\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n88\tchapter: \"4.3.5\"\n89\ttopic: main training regime for all-round sports\n90\tcategory: [multi-event, programming, regime-of-work]\n91\tconfidence: untested\n92\tingest-tier: full-text\n93\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n94\t---\n95\t\n96\t# For All-Round Athletes, Cyclic and Phasic-Tonic Regimes Are Probably the Most Important, a Conclusion the Authors Say Still Needs Experimental Proof\n97\t\n98\t**What it is** — Choosing a regime of work is hard for one motor action and harder \"in all-round sports like decathlon, gymnastics and modern pentathlon.\" The authors report that \"Almost one hundred years of experience in training all-round track athletes indicates that their fundamental training regime is speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type,\" \"corroborated by using statistical analysis to identify the key exercises in the 'all-round' complex (Zatsiorsky, 1966; Lukauskas, 1967; Mamdzhanyan, 1976).\" Gymnasts, they say, long trained primarily statically with some dynamic work such as jumping, until rising standards and elements such as \"jumping with extensive longitudinal rotation\" made them reconsider \"in favour of dynamic muscular tension.\" Their conclusion: \"cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably should be regarded as the most important in all-round training,\" with other regimes added for the skills of the specific sport; \"However, this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.)\n99\t\n100\t**Why it works** — The authors give coaching history and statistical identification of key exercises as support, and no physiological mechanism. They label the conclusion \"probably\" and say it lacks rigorous experimental support, which is why this entry is `untested`.\n101\t\n102\t**When to apply** — Inference: as a starting hypothesis for multi-quality athletes whose events mix running, jumping and throwing: give the base strength work a cyclic, explosive-dynamic character, then add the regimes that specific events require. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182). Inference: the authors' \"main regime\" question is one way to decide which compatible qualities to train together in a multi-quality athlete.\n103\t\n104\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors' own caveat governs: \"this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\" The cited statistical work (1966-1976) is not described in the text, so its samples and methods cannot be checked here. \"Phasic-tonic\" is not defined in this passage. Do not apply it to single-event athletes, for whom the authors' rule is to match the specific action's regime instead.\n105\t\n106\t**Example** — The book's own: decathletes (speed-cyclic, explosive dynamic regime) and gymnasts moving from static to dynamic training (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n107\t\n108\t**Related** — [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]] (Ch2.3.4, key abilities in a sport's fitness structure).\n109\t=================== heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means.md\n110\t---\n111\ttype: library-entry\n112\tkind: heuristic\n113\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n114\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n115\tchapter: \"4.3.6\"\n116\ttopic: movement analysis for exercise selection\n117\tcategory: [biomechanics, video-analysis, force-plate, exercise-selection]\n118\tconfidence: supported\n119\tingest-tier: full-text\n120\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n121\t---\n122\t\n123\t# Select Special Strength Means from a Phase-by-Phase Analysis of the Sport Movement, Ideally Video Synchronised with Force Data\n124\t\n125\t**What it is** — Under \"4.3.6 Correspondence of Training Means to the Sports Movements,\" the authors say the understanding of muscular work \"is derived from analysis of the athlete's movements,\" most conveniently \"a high speed video system to record and analyse each movement at intervals separated by fractions of a second\" (PDF pp. 258-259 / printed pp. 247-248). The kinematic structure \"helps to identify those actions which play either a fundamental or supportive role in determining motor skill,\" and knowledge is \"more precise if it is based on quantitative information which can be obtained only from computerised video analysis.\" Analysis can use \"the phasic structure of the movement (according to Donskoi)\": the act is divided into phases \"in which the direction of movement, the application of force and the character of muscular work are distinguished,\" and the picture \"will be more complete if forces are simultaneously recorded along with the video images (e.g. using a force plate or cable tensiometer).\" Because \"full reproduction of the complex interaction of the different types of fitness strength is not always possible in training,\" it becomes necessary \"to exercise locally the interacting muscle groups that are crucial for controlling specific movements,\" based on how the kinesiological structure forms (see 2.1.2, 2.1.3). Its elements develop with \"heterochronicity\" (see 2.2.3): elements interacting directly with external objects develop faster than those interacting indirectly, and \"relatively harmonious development of its elements\" requires \"an organised system of special strength training.\" The section ends: \"success in selecting the means of special strength training is determined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements.\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248.)\n126\t\n127\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: the five criteria can only be checked against a movement whose phases, force directions and work regimes are known, and quantitative records make that knowledge precise. They give no evidence that analysed selection outperforms unanalysed selection.\n128\t\n129\t**When to apply** — Before building a special strength menu for a skill: film it at high frame rate, split it into phases, and for each phase note movement direction, force application and type of muscular work; add force-plate data where possible. Then choose local exercises for the muscle groups that control the critical phases. Eric's baseline: \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 33). Inference: the authors' video-plus-force analysis is the \"gather data\" step that follows the coach's eye.\n130\t\n131\t**When NOT to apply** — The claim that precise knowledge \"can be obtained only from computerised video analysis\" is the authors' judgment, not a tested finding, and dates from the book's era of equipment. The heterochronicity point is a cross-reference to Ch2 rather than new evidence. Inference: a full phase analysis is costly; for a team roster it may only be practical for a few key skills.\n132\t\n133\t**Example** — The book gives no worked example in 4.3.6. Inference: a running back's cut could be split into the approach, the plant (eccentric braking), and the push-off (concentric), with a force plate under the plant foot showing the force direction in each phase.\n134\t\n135\t**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--kinesiological-pattern-and-strength-field-components]] (Ch2.1.3), [[framework--kinematic-pair-chain-system-movement-analysis-levels]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]] (Ch2.2.4), [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/heuristic--emg-timing-shortlists-exercises-it-does-not-prove-transfer]] (Moyer: phase analysis shortlists exercises but does not prove transfer; a caution this section does not state).\n136\t=================== claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance.md\n137\t---\n138\ttype: library-entry\n139\tkind: claim\n140\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n141\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n142\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n143\ttopic: VO2 max and endurance performance\n144\tcategory: [endurance, aerobic, testing, monitoring]\n145\tconfidence: supported\n146\tingest-tier: full-text\n147\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n148\t---\n149\t\n150\t# VO2 Max on Its Own Does Not Decide Endurance Results: Equal VO2 Max, Different Results; Stable VO2 Max, Rising Results; Falling VO2 Max in the Competition Period\n151\t\n152\t**What it is** — The authors argue that analysing endurance \"solely at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate,\" especially when endurance is reduced to respiratory function and VO2 max, and that endurance methods \"are still based on such ideas\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248). Three groups of evidence:\n153\t1. Athletes with equal VO2 max show different results, and the reverse. \"Top middle-distance runners of the 1940s had the same VO2 max as today's athletes, yet there are major differences in performance (Faulkner, 1968).\" There are \"no statistically reliable differences between athletes of different qualification\" in aerobic potential, but \"significant differences with respect to anaerobic efficiency\" (Nabotnikova 1972; Serafimova 1974; Rusko 1976).\n154\t2. VO2 max stabilises in highly qualified athletes while results improve: the best Soviet and Swedish skaters over 4-5 years (Orlov & Sharova 1977); qualified road cyclists over many years (Melenberg et al 1972).\n155\t3. VO2 max falls significantly in the competition period in cyclists, swimmers, skiers, skaters, middle-distance runners and hockey players (citations for each, PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248), and its correlation with results falls from \"R = 0.7 during the preparation period\" to \"0.4 during the competitive period\" (Vasiliev & Trunin 1974; Melenberg 1981; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image).\n156\t\n157\tThey conclude \"VO2 max on its own is no guarantee of outstanding performance,\" and that other factors count, \"such as the ability to perform work for a prolonged period at a level of oxygen consumption close to the VO2 max, and the ability to effectively utilise available energy under competitive conditions\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n158\t\n159\t**Why it works** — For the competition-period fall in VO2 max the authors offer two possible explanations, \"the decreasing trainability of aerobic processes or ... an inherent antagonism between aerobic and anaerobic processes (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Volkov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249). One page later they say the antagonism assumption \"must be questioned\" (Mellenberg & Khvan 1982; PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250) and explain the fall through blood-flow changes instead (see [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]]). Both are kept here because the text gives both.\n160\t\n161\t**When to apply** — When a coach or test battery treats VO2 max as the main endurance marker: also measure sustained work near VO2 max, anaerobic capacity, and sport-specific work capacity, and do not read a stable or falling VO2 max in season as lost fitness by itself. Eric's baseline: conditioning is defined as meeting the sport's energy requirements and sustaining \"strength, speed, and power output across a full competition.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113). Inference: that definition already points away from a single aerobic marker, in line with this section.\n162\t\n163\t**When NOT to apply** — The evidence is a list of mostly Soviet citations from 1968-1981 summarised in a sentence each, with no sample sizes or methods given, and the correlation drop (0.7 to 0.4) is reported without the sport or sample. The 1940s comparison mixes eras with different training, tracks and competition depth. This does not show VO2 max is unimportant: the authors themselves recommend raising it (see the contested entry in Related).\n164\t\n165\t**Example** — The book's own: qualified road cyclists whose VO2 max stayed \"practically unchanged ... over many years of training, yet their performances improved\" (Melenberg et al 1972; PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248).\n166\t\n167\t**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (Table 4.2, aerobic factors' share falls at Master of Sport level), [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]].\n168\t=================== claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md\n169\t---\n170\ttype: library-entry\n171\tkind: claim\n172\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n173\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n174\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n175\ttopic: muscular basis of endurance\n176\tcategory: [endurance, lactate, metabolism, strength-endurance]\n177\tconfidence: contested\n178\tingest-tier: full-text\n179\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n180\t---\n181\t\n182\t# The Authors Locate Endurance in the Muscles' Oxidative and Strength Qualities Rather Than in VO2 Max, but Give Conflicting Advice on Glycolytic Work\n183\t\n184\t**What it is** — Under \"Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image), the authors cite research that endurance gains are \"associated more with enhancing the ability of the muscles to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the amount of oxygen in the bloodstream\" (Ekblom 1969; Rowell 1971; Saltin 1974). \"A rise in intramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the muscles decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (four citations), and lactate falls \"in the skeletal muscles and not in the liver and myocardium, as has always been believed\" (Jorfeldt 1970; Knutten 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen 1973). Their italicised conclusion: \"the development of endurance is associated with functional specialisation of the skeletal muscles, particularly the enhancement of their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement of cardiorespiratory ability.\" So the emphasis should be \"to decrease the proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise lactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate,\" aiming \"chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities of the muscles.\"\n185\t\n186\t**Why it works** — The mechanism the authors give: stronger, more oxidative muscle relies less on glycolysis, makes less lactate and oxidises more of it in the muscle itself. They support it with citations, not with data shown in the text.\n187\t\n188\t**When to apply** — When planning endurance or repeat-effort conditioning: include strength work and work that develops the working muscles' oxidative capacity, not only central (heart-lung) work. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning is a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice [**Explicit**, line 116], though \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" [**Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**, line 117]. Inference: the authors' \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" line supports the first; their own p.249 and p.253 statements (below) support the off-season glycolytic work.\n189\t\n190\t**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: the authors' own pages pull in opposite directions.\n191\t- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n192\t- Glycolytic work, more or less: \"it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249) and, for skaters, \"it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253), against \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n193\t\n194\tInference: the passages may describe different aims (a VO2 max reserve before the season; muscle-level economy as the main target), but the text does not reconcile them. Also unclear on the same page: \"Anaerobic work is approximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's advantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include anaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); the \"therefore\" does not follow clearly from the 50% figure, and no measure of \"effective\" is given. The sources are 1950-1975 and are not described.\n195\t\n196\t**Example** — The book gives no training example in this passage. Inference: a winger's conditioning block that adds heavy lower-body strength work and longer threshold-paced intervals, rather than only more aerobic volume, follows the authors' main line.\n197\t\n198\t**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths]] (Ch1.15, lactate is not a toxin; agreement on a muscle-centred view of lactate), [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (the p.253 glycolytic statement), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]].\n199\t=================== claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md\n200\t---\n201\ttype: library-entry\n202\tkind: claim\n203\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n204\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n205\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n206\ttopic: haemodynamics and competition-period endurance\n207\tcategory: [endurance, blood-flow, periodization, aerobic]\n208\tconfidence: supported\n209\tingest-tier: full-text\n210\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n211\t---\n212\t\n213\t# In the Competition Period, Local Blood Flow to the Working Muscles Rises While VO2 Max Falls, and the Authors Credit Endurance Gains to the Blood Flow\n214\t\n215\t**What it is** — \"Recently, haemodynamic factors have been shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work\" (nine citations). In italics: \"The redistribution of blood and an increase in circulation to the working muscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements of the muscles and the removal of anaerobic metabolites.\" This \"Differentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow\" (Inference: \"vesicular\" is probably meant as \"vascular\") develops in the competition period \"as a result of the extensive aerobic loading executed during the preparatory period.\" In 25 km road cycling, improved performance came with a fall in VO2 max in the competition period while \"peak blood flow to the lower extremities increases,\" and cardiac output also fell (Stepochkin et al 1970). (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image.) \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\": while VO2 max falls somewhat in the competition period, \"OBLA can rise\" (Zatsiorsky et al 1974; Sirenko 1979; Nurmekivan 1974). In qualified skaters the mean change in VO2 max in the competition period is \"5-10%,\" while local blood flow rises \"much more significantly (50-250%)\" (Mellenberg 1981). The authors conclude that training effectiveness \"does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport,\" and that, with the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities, \"the redistribution of bloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions for developing local muscle endurance.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.) Because these conditions lessen dependence on VO2 max, the assumed \"antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" (PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250).\n216\t\n217\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: more blood to the working muscles meets their oxygen need and clears anaerobic metabolites, so less glycolysis is needed, which also explains why VO2 max can fall without harm. They support it by citation; the text gives no figures beyond those above.\n218\t\n219\t**When to apply** — When interpreting in-season test results: a flat or falling VO2 max during the competition period need not mean lost endurance if threshold measures and performance hold. Build the aerobic base in the preparatory period, since the authors credit the competition-period blood-flow change to that loading. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery ... not distance capacity.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123). Inference: local blood-flow capacity is a plausible route for that recovery, but the book discusses endurance athletes, not repeat-sprint sports.\n220\t\n221\t**When NOT to apply** — A sentence on the same page is unclear: \"blood lactate concentration can increase by some 6% as a result of training, while the VO2 max level increases by only 7% (Williams et al, 1967)\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250; the image confirms 6% and 7%). As printed, 7% is larger than 6%, so \"only\" does not fit; the intended figure or measure (perhaps the lactate threshold rather than lactate concentration) cannot be recovered from the text. Do not cite it. The 50-250% blood-flow range is wide and given without method. The antagonism point is the authors developing an argument across two pages (offered as a possible explanation on p.249, questioned on p.250), not settled.\n222\t\n223\t**Example** — The book's own: 25 km road cyclists whose performance improved while VO2 max and cardiac output fell and leg blood flow rose (Stepochkin et al 1970; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n224\t\n225\t**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]].\n226\t"}]},"uuid":"b886d86e-fb0e-415b-b1bc-21e2b811abab","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:40:20.074Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e2.txt","content":"=================== heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.4\"\ntopic: rate and time of force production criterion\ncategory: [rate-of-force-development, explosive-strength, specificity, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Use the Rate of Force Development as a Correspondence Criterion; Do Not Use Impulse to Compare Different Movements\n\n**What it is** — The criterion of correspondence for \"the rate of developing maximum force\" complements the dynamic-effort criterion and \"is particularly important in those sporting activities requiring explosive strength\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). \"The ability to increase power or explosive strength also depends on specifically training the RFD factor,\" which the authors call \"vital\" to programme design for any sport with explosive actions, and they name plyometric training as \"one of the practical consequences\" of this analysis. Explosive strength may be evaluated by power, impulse (F.Δt) or the Reactivity Coefficient (RC = Fmax/tmax.mg = RFDmax/W), \"although the significance of each of them can be different\": impulse \"is acceptable only when it is not necessary to compare different movements\"; comparing movements would be valid \"only if the time taken is identical for each movement, a situation that does not occur in human movement,\" so for that purpose \"determination of the impulse of the force is irrelevant\" (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246). Where effort lasts only briefly, \"if it is necessary for the athlete to produce a large force within some time limit, he must do it quickly and use a high RFD,\" a necessity set by the activity and \"especially the working amplitude of the movement.\" RFD \"is an important criterion of correspondence ... which relates directly to the characteristics of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"all training is primarily neuromuscular and ... training based entirely on variables such as exercise choice, load, sets and repetitions is highly inadequate for producing sport specific fitness\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: when the time available is limited by the movement's amplitude, how fast force rises decides how much force can be applied; and impulse depends on duration, so two movements of different duration cannot be compared on it. They tie RFD to \"the characteristics of the neuromuscular system\" without further mechanism here.\n\n**When to apply** — When an action has a short force-application window (jumps, sprints, throws, blocking contact): pick exercises and tests that reproduce the rate of force rise, not just peak force; use RFD or RC rather than impulse when comparing different exercises or tests. Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing is the daily readiness marker [**Explicit**, `docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74], and the CMJ family is run on ForceDecks [**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**, line 77]. Inference: mRSI is a time-normalised jump index, so it fits this criterion's preference for rate over impulse; the book does not discuss mRSI.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors' rejection of impulse is scoped to comparing different movements; they accept it within one movement. Their statement that training built only on exercise choice, load, sets and repetitions is \"highly inadequate\" is a strong judgment without cited data. The RC formula repeats the 2.2.1 definition; the book defines RC in more than one way elsewhere (see [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]), so check which form a source means.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: plyometric training as the practical consequence of treating RFD as a trainable factor (PDF p. 257 / printed p. 246).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--explosive-strength-deficit-and-rate-of-force-development-indices]].\n=================== heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.5\"\ntopic: regime of muscular work criterion\ncategory: [specificity, programming, load-selection, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Same Exercise Serves Different Sports Only If Its Regime Changes: Load, Tempo, Sets and Repetitions Must Match the Sport's Work\n\n**What it is** — The fifth correspondence criterion \"relies on determining the character of the muscular work involved,\" and the regime \"should be taken into consideration for selecting the means and methods of special strength training,\" because \"depending upon the character of its execution, the same means can solve different tasks.\" The authors' example: \"boxers and shot putters need to execute the same exercise (e.g. performing a thrusting arm movement against the resistance of a pulley machine) with different weights, tempos, number of sets and repetitions, because boxing requires primarily a quick, unresisted movement and the ability to repeat it many times without significant decrease in force, whereas shotputting involves rapid single movements against a specific resistance.\" The problem has two tasks: \"selection of the regime for a specific motor action (e.g. the key elements in an exercise)\" and \"selection of the main regime which will best develop all of the diverse muscular activities in all-round training\"; the second \"is still far from being resolved.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is that the training effect is set by how an exercise is executed, not by the exercise's name or shape, so the regime parameters must reproduce the sport's demand (repeated fast unresisted actions for the boxer, single fast actions against a set resistance for the shot putter). They give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — After an exercise passes the movement criteria (amplitude, direction, accentuated region), set its loading parameters from the sport's regime: repeated low-resistance fast actions call for light loads, fast tempo and many repetitions; single maximal actions against a fixed implement call for heavier loads, few repetitions and full recovery. Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of 'sport specific.'\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102). Inference: this criterion adds that position on that spectrum depends on how an exercise is loaded, not only which exercise it is.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book gives the boxer and shot putter contrast without numbers, so it does not prescribe specific loads. For all-round sports (decathlon, gymnastics, modern pentathlon) the authors say the choice of a main regime is unresolved (see [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]). Regime matching does not replace the other four criteria.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: a pulley-machine thrusting arm movement done by a boxer (light, fast, many repetitions) and by a shot putter (heavier, single fast efforts) (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]], [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]], [[heuristic--choice-of-muscle-training-regime-favor-combination]] (4.2, choice among isometric, eccentric and other regimes), [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]] (Evans applies the regime criterion to a wrestling takedown).\n=================== claim--regime-switching-ability-decides-mixed-regime-events.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.5\"\ntopic: switching between regimes of work\ncategory: [specificity, jumping, technique, special-strength]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In Mixed-Regime Events, the Ability to Switch Quickly from One Regime of Muscular Work to Another Decides the Result\n\n**What it is** — \"In conjunction with perfecting motor skills in a specific activity, one should consider the importance of changing from one activity to another in those exercises where this is necessary.\" The authors' examples: \"an athlete's results in the long jump are determined mainly by the ability to switch from the cyclic regime of muscular work in running, to explosive effort in the take-off,\" and \"good gymnasts are capable of switching rapidly from dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension.\" \"The methodology underlying the development of these complex capabilities is the aim of technical training in all sport. Its resolution is the goal of special strength training.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.) In 4.3.2 the authors also place the peak of force in mixed-regime movements \"at the instant of switching from one regime to another\" (PDF p. 255 / printed p. 244).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the long-jump \"determined mainly by\" statement. Inference: read with 4.3.2, the switch is where peak force falls, so a weak transition would cost force at the moment it matters most; this link is drawn here, not in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — For events that chain regimes (run-up into take-off, approach into a plant, a sprint into a cut, a dynamic move into a held position): include special strength work that reproduces the transition itself, not only each regime separately. Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence, including \"regime of muscular work,\" governs transfer. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: in football, a block or tackle that starts from a run and ends in a held isometric contest is a regime switch of the kind described; the book does not discuss football.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The long-jump claim (\"determined mainly by\") has no cited study or data in the text. The authors assign the problem to both technical training and special strength training without saying how to split the work, so this is a direction, not a method.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: the long jumper switching from cyclic running to explosive take-off; the gymnast switching from dynamic explosive effort to isometric tension (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]] (4.3.2, the switch as the force peak), [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]].\n=================== claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.5\"\ntopic: main training regime for all-round sports\ncategory: [multi-event, programming, regime-of-work]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# For All-Round Athletes, Cyclic and Phasic-Tonic Regimes Are Probably the Most Important, a Conclusion the Authors Say Still Needs Experimental Proof\n\n**What it is** — Choosing a regime of work is hard for one motor action and harder \"in all-round sports like decathlon, gymnastics and modern pentathlon.\" The authors report that \"Almost one hundred years of experience in training all-round track athletes indicates that their fundamental training regime is speed-cyclic with dynamic tension, primarily of the explosive type,\" \"corroborated by using statistical analysis to identify the key exercises in the 'all-round' complex (Zatsiorsky, 1966; Lukauskas, 1967; Mamdzhanyan, 1976).\" Gymnasts, they say, long trained primarily statically with some dynamic work such as jumping, until rising standards and elements such as \"jumping with extensive longitudinal rotation\" made them reconsider \"in favour of dynamic muscular tension.\" Their conclusion: \"cyclic and phasic-tonic regimes probably should be regarded as the most important in all-round training,\" with other regimes added for the skills of the specific sport; \"However, this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\" (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give coaching history and statistical identification of key exercises as support, and no physiological mechanism. They label the conclusion \"probably\" and say it lacks rigorous experimental support, which is why this entry is `untested`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: as a starting hypothesis for multi-quality athletes whose events mix running, jumping and throwing: give the base strength work a cyclic, explosive-dynamic character, then add the regimes that specific events require. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182). Inference: the authors' \"main regime\" question is one way to decide which compatible qualities to train together in a multi-quality athlete.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors' own caveat governs: \"this conclusion needs rigorous experimental substantiation.\" The cited statistical work (1966-1976) is not described in the text, so its samples and methods cannot be checked here. \"Phasic-tonic\" is not defined in this passage. Do not apply it to single-event athletes, for whom the authors' rule is to match the specific action's regime instead.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: decathletes (speed-cyclic, explosive dynamic regime) and gymnasts moving from static to dynamic training (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]] (Ch2.3.4, key abilities in a sport's fitness structure).\n=================== heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3.6\"\ntopic: movement analysis for exercise selection\ncategory: [biomechanics, video-analysis, force-plate, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Select Special Strength Means from a Phase-by-Phase Analysis of the Sport Movement, Ideally Video Synchronised with Force Data\n\n**What it is** — Under \"4.3.6 Correspondence of Training Means to the Sports Movements,\" the authors say the understanding of muscular work \"is derived from analysis of the athlete's movements,\" most conveniently \"a high speed video system to record and analyse each movement at intervals separated by fractions of a second\" (PDF pp. 258-259 / printed pp. 247-248). The kinematic structure \"helps to identify those actions which play either a fundamental or supportive role in determining motor skill,\" and knowledge is \"more precise if it is based on quantitative information which can be obtained only from computerised video analysis.\" Analysis can use \"the phasic structure of the movement (according to Donskoi)\": the act is divided into phases \"in which the direction of movement, the application of force and the character of muscular work are distinguished,\" and the picture \"will be more complete if forces are simultaneously recorded along with the video images (e.g. using a force plate or cable tensiometer).\" Because \"full reproduction of the complex interaction of the different types of fitness strength is not always possible in training,\" it becomes necessary \"to exercise locally the interacting muscle groups that are crucial for controlling specific movements,\" based on how the kinesiological structure forms (see 2.1.2, 2.1.3). Its elements develop with \"heterochronicity\" (see 2.2.3): elements interacting directly with external objects develop faster than those interacting indirectly, and \"relatively harmonious development of its elements\" requires \"an organised system of special strength training.\" The section ends: \"success in selecting the means of special strength training is determined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements.\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning: the five criteria can only be checked against a movement whose phases, force directions and work regimes are known, and quantitative records make that knowledge precise. They give no evidence that analysed selection outperforms unanalysed selection.\n\n**When to apply** — Before building a special strength menu for a skill: film it at high frame rate, split it into phases, and for each phase note movement direction, force application and type of muscular work; add force-plate data where possible. Then choose local exercises for the muscle groups that control the critical phases. Eric's baseline: \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 33). Inference: the authors' video-plus-force analysis is the \"gather data\" step that follows the coach's eye.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The claim that precise knowledge \"can be obtained only from computerised video analysis\" is the authors' judgment, not a tested finding, and dates from the book's era of equipment. The heterochronicity point is a cross-reference to Ch2 rather than new evidence. Inference: a full phase analysis is costly; for a team roster it may only be practical for a few key skills.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no worked example in 4.3.6. Inference: a running back's cut could be split into the approach, the plant (eccentric braking), and the push-off (concentric), with a force plate under the plant foot showing the force direction in each phase.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--kinesiological-pattern-and-strength-field-components]] (Ch2.1.3), [[framework--kinematic-pair-chain-system-movement-analysis-levels]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]] (Ch2.2.4), [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/heuristic--emg-timing-shortlists-exercises-it-does-not-prove-transfer]] (Moyer: phase analysis shortlists exercises but does not prove transfer; a caution this section does not state).\n=================== claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: VO2 max and endurance performance\ncategory: [endurance, aerobic, testing, monitoring]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# VO2 Max on Its Own Does Not Decide Endurance Results: Equal VO2 Max, Different Results; Stable VO2 Max, Rising Results; Falling VO2 Max in the Competition Period\n\n**What it is** — The authors argue that analysing endurance \"solely at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems is inadequate,\" especially when endurance is reduced to respiratory function and VO2 max, and that endurance methods \"are still based on such ideas\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248). Three groups of evidence:\n1. Athletes with equal VO2 max show different results, and the reverse. \"Top middle-distance runners of the 1940s had the same VO2 max as today's athletes, yet there are major differences in performance (Faulkner, 1968).\" There are \"no statistically reliable differences between athletes of different qualification\" in aerobic potential, but \"significant differences with respect to anaerobic efficiency\" (Nabotnikova 1972; Serafimova 1974; Rusko 1976).\n2. VO2 max stabilises in highly qualified athletes while results improve: the best Soviet and Swedish skaters over 4-5 years (Orlov & Sharova 1977); qualified road cyclists over many years (Melenberg et al 1972).\n3. VO2 max falls significantly in the competition period in cyclists, swimmers, skiers, skaters, middle-distance runners and hockey players (citations for each, PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248), and its correlation with results falls from \"R = 0.7 during the preparation period\" to \"0.4 during the competitive period\" (Vasiliev & Trunin 1974; Melenberg 1981; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image).\n\nThey conclude \"VO2 max on its own is no guarantee of outstanding performance,\" and that other factors count, \"such as the ability to perform work for a prolonged period at a level of oxygen consumption close to the VO2 max, and the ability to effectively utilise available energy under competitive conditions\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n\n**Why it works** — For the competition-period fall in VO2 max the authors offer two possible explanations, \"the decreasing trainability of aerobic processes or ... an inherent antagonism between aerobic and anaerobic processes (Zatsiorsky et al, 1974; Volkov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249). One page later they say the antagonism assumption \"must be questioned\" (Mellenberg & Khvan 1982; PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250) and explain the fall through blood-flow changes instead (see [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]]). Both are kept here because the text gives both.\n\n**When to apply** — When a coach or test battery treats VO2 max as the main endurance marker: also measure sustained work near VO2 max, anaerobic capacity, and sport-specific work capacity, and do not read a stable or falling VO2 max in season as lost fitness by itself. Eric's baseline: conditioning is defined as meeting the sport's energy requirements and sustaining \"strength, speed, and power output across a full competition.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 113). Inference: that definition already points away from a single aerobic marker, in line with this section.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The evidence is a list of mostly Soviet citations from 1968-1981 summarised in a sentence each, with no sample sizes or methods given, and the correlation drop (0.7 to 0.4) is reported without the sport or sample. The 1940s comparison mixes eras with different training, tracks and competition depth. This does not show VO2 max is unimportant: the authors themselves recommend raising it (see the contested entry in Related).\n\n**Example** — The book's own: qualified road cyclists whose VO2 max stayed \"practically unchanged ... over many years of training, yet their performances improved\" (Melenberg et al 1972; PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (Table 4.2, aerobic factors' share falls at Master of Sport level), [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]].\n=================== claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: muscular basis of endurance\ncategory: [endurance, lactate, metabolism, strength-endurance]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Authors Locate Endurance in the Muscles' Oxidative and Strength Qualities Rather Than in VO2 Max, but Give Conflicting Advice on Glycolytic Work\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image), the authors cite research that endurance gains are \"associated more with enhancing the ability of the muscles to utilise a higher percentage of the oxygen already in the blood than with increasing the amount of oxygen in the bloodstream\" (Ekblom 1969; Rowell 1971; Saltin 1974). \"A rise in intramuscular energy potential, the power of the oxidative processes and the contractile (strength) qualities of the muscles decrease the rate of glycolysis\" (four citations), and lactate falls \"in the skeletal muscles and not in the liver and myocardium, as has always been believed\" (Jorfeldt 1970; Knutten 1971; Gollnick & Hermansen 1973). Their italicised conclusion: \"the development of endurance is associated with functional specialisation of the skeletal muscles, particularly the enhancement of their strength and oxidative qualities, rather than improvement of cardiorespiratory ability.\" So the emphasis should be \"to decrease the proportion of glycolysis in supplying the energy for work and improve the ability of the muscles to oxidise lactate during work, and not simply to improve accommodation to high concentrations of blood lactate,\" aiming \"chiefly at eliminating the disparity between the anaerobic and aerobic abilities of the muscles.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The mechanism the authors give: stronger, more oxidative muscle relies less on glycolysis, makes less lactate and oxidises more of it in the muscle itself. They support it with citations, not with data shown in the text.\n\n**When to apply** — When planning endurance or repeat-effort conditioning: include strength work and work that develops the working muscles' oxidative capacity, not only central (heart-lung) work. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning is a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice [**Explicit**, line 116], though \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" [**Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**, line 117]. Inference: the authors' \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" line supports the first; their own p.249 and p.253 statements (below) support the off-season glycolytic work.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: the authors' own pages pull in opposite directions.\n- Raise VO2 max, or not: \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max, and in particular, to create some reserve of aerobic power by the end of the preparatory period\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249), against \"effectiveness of training does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n- Glycolytic work, more or less: \"it is advisable to execute a specific part of the training load with a high blood lactate concentration\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249) and, for skaters, \"it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253), against \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n\nInference: the passages may describe different aims (a VO2 max reserve before the season; muscle-level economy as the main target), but the text does not reconcile them. Also unclear on the same page: \"Anaerobic work is approximately 50% more effective than aerobic work in raising this lactate threshold, therefore it is to the athlete's advantage to execute prolonged, intense work without the preliminary accumulation of lactate and include anaerobic energy production (Christensen & Hogberg, 1950)\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); the \"therefore\" does not follow clearly from the 50% figure, and no measure of \"effective\" is given. The sources are 1950-1975 and are not described.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no training example in this passage. Inference: a winger's conditioning block that adds heavy lower-body strength work and longer threshold-paced intervals, rather than only more aerobic volume, follows the authors' main line.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[claim--lactate-and-oxygen-debt-myths]] (Ch1.15, lactate is not a toxin; agreement on a muscle-centred view of lactate), [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]] (the p.253 glycolytic statement), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]].\n=================== claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: haemodynamics and competition-period endurance\ncategory: [endurance, blood-flow, periodization, aerobic]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# In the Competition Period, Local Blood Flow to the Working Muscles Rises While VO2 Max Falls, and the Authors Credit Endurance Gains to the Blood Flow\n\n**What it is** — \"Recently, haemodynamic factors have been shown to play an important role in the functional specialisation of the body during endurance work\" (nine citations). In italics: \"The redistribution of blood and an increase in circulation to the working muscles contributes to satisfying the oxygen requirements of the muscles and the removal of anaerobic metabolites.\" This \"Differentiation of the vesicular reaction providing effective redistribution of blood flow\" (Inference: \"vesicular\" is probably meant as \"vascular\") develops in the competition period \"as a result of the extensive aerobic loading executed during the preparatory period.\" In 25 km road cycling, improved performance came with a fall in VO2 max in the competition period while \"peak blood flow to the lower extremities increases,\" and cardiac output also fell (Stepochkin et al 1970). (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249, verified against the page image.) \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\": while VO2 max falls somewhat in the competition period, \"OBLA can rise\" (Zatsiorsky et al 1974; Sirenko 1979; Nurmekivan 1974). In qualified skaters the mean change in VO2 max in the competition period is \"5-10%,\" while local blood flow rises \"much more significantly (50-250%)\" (Mellenberg 1981). The authors conclude that training effectiveness \"does not lie in raising VO2 max, but in enhancing the haemodynamic efficiency of oxygen transport,\" and that, with the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities, \"the redistribution of bloodflow and improved local vesicular reaction are important conditions for developing local muscle endurance.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.) Because these conditions lessen dependence on VO2 max, the assumed \"antagonism in endurance sport between the development of the aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms must be questioned (Mellenberg & Khvan, 1982)\" (PDF pp. 260-261 / printed pp. 249-250).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: more blood to the working muscles meets their oxygen need and clears anaerobic metabolites, so less glycolysis is needed, which also explains why VO2 max can fall without harm. They support it by citation; the text gives no figures beyond those above.\n\n**When to apply** — When interpreting in-season test results: a flat or falling VO2 max during the competition period need not mean lost endurance if threshold measures and performance hold. Build the aerobic base in the preparatory period, since the authors credit the competition-period blood-flow change to that loading. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery ... not distance capacity.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123). Inference: local blood-flow capacity is a plausible route for that recovery, but the book discusses endurance athletes, not repeat-sprint sports.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — A sentence on the same page is unclear: \"blood lactate concentration can increase by some 6% as a result of training, while the VO2 max level increases by only 7% (Williams et al, 1967)\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250; the image confirms 6% and 7%). As printed, 7% is larger than 6%, so \"only\" does not fit; the intended figure or measure (perhaps the lactate threshold rather than lactate concentration) cannot be recovered from the text. Do not cite it. The 50-250% blood-flow range is wide and given without method. The antagonism point is the authors developing an argument across two pages (offered as a possible explanation on p.249, questioned on p.250), not settled.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: 25 km road cyclists whose performance improved while VO2 max and cardiac output fell and leg blood flow rose (Stepochkin et al 1970; PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]].\n","numLines":226,"startLine":1,"totalLines":226}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d417d493-e991-448b-b7ae-3a9c5a3d7cc2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
