{"parentUuid":"d1add111-7c93-4514-b448-a83cdecee14a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffUdhgtPH1VdrtvCujR5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XqeReoJbL3CPFJGmxiLpH6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e3.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12604,"cache_read_input_tokens":130061,"output_tokens":2287,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2173},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12604},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2287,"cache_read_input_tokens":130061,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12604,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12604,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XqeReoJbL3CPFJGmxiLpH6":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e3.txt"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffUda22MnqMduh2vnSkf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"53cf932f-e3c1-41d5-af1b-cfa73174f9e7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:40:44.122Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"53cf932f-e3c1-41d5-af1b-cfa73174f9e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqeReoJbL3CPFJGmxiLpH6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=================== principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles.md\n2\t---\n3\ttype: library-entry\n4\tkind: principle\n5\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n6\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n7\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n8\ttopic: local specificity of endurance adaptation\n9\tcategory: [endurance, specificity, conditioning, cross-training]\n10\tconfidence: supported\n11\tingest-tier: full-text\n12\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t# Anaerobic and Local Endurance Adaptations Stay in the Muscles That Were Trained: General Conditioning Raises VO2 Max but Not Sport-Specific Work Capacity\n16\t\n17\t**What it is** — \"Sports exercises are performed by specific muscle groups and if metabolic processes in these muscles are especially intense, then the products of anaerobic metabolism accumulate, resulting in fatigue and decreased work capacity. Therefore, adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature.\" Training different muscle groups can give \"approximately equivalent functional effect at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory systems,\" but \"it is displayed only in specific situations, i.e. while using those same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972).\" For skiers, \"running and imitation skiing without poles raises the general functional level during the preparatory period, including VO2 max, but does not develop the specific physical preparedness necessary for speed of movement on skis,\" whereas \"imitation skiing and indoor skiing on rollers with poles enhances local muscular endurance and vesicular reactions in the muscles of the upper extremities, which leads to the efficient redistribution of blood flow and an increase in specific work-capacity for skiing (Yevstratov et al, 1975).\" The authors define local muscular endurance as \"the athlete's ability to produce the strength component of movement for a prolonged period without significant fatigue,\" and say \"practical ways of developing local muscular endurance still need serious research\"; \"one should find ways to intensify muscular work in those regimes characterising the given sport and utilise special strength exercises for this purpose (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.)\n18\t\n19\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: fatigue products build up in the specific muscles doing intense work, and the blood-flow and local endurance adaptations develop in those same muscles, so a central gain trained with other muscles does not carry the local part.\n20\t\n21\t**When to apply** — When choosing conditioning modes: if the sport's limiting fatigue is local (specific muscle groups under repeated intense effort), condition with the sport's muscles in the sport's regime, using special strength exercises, not only general running or cycling. Eric's baseline: \"'Everything we do is some form of conditioning': lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system ... and should be classified and counted as conditioning.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122). Inference: this principle supports counting position-specific drills as conditioning, because general running would not build the same local capacity.\n22\t\n23\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors grant that general work still raises the general functional level and VO2 max in the preparatory period; this is not an argument to drop it. The principle concerns local (anaerobic, muscular) endurance; the cardiorespiratory gain from other muscle groups is described as roughly equivalent. The citations are summarised without data.\n24\t\n25\t**Example** — The book's own: skiers who run or imitate skiing without poles (VO2 max rises, ski speed does not) against skiers who roller-ski with poles (upper-limb local endurance and specific work capacity rise) (Yevstratov et al 1975; PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n26\t\n27\t**Related** — [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]] (the authors' caution on generalised cross training), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, general versus local strength-endurance), [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (metabolism and fatigue as specificity dimensions).\n28\t=================== claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md\n29\t---\n30\ttype: library-entry\n31\tkind: claim\n32\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n33\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n34\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n35\ttopic: 400 m stride length and strength-endurance\n36\tcategory: [sprinting, strength-endurance, fatigue, speed-endurance]\n37\tconfidence: supported\n38\tingest-tier: full-text\n39\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n40\t---\n41\t\n42\t# Faster 400 m Runners Hold Stride Length Through Local Strength-Endurance; Under Fatigue, Stride Length Falls More Than Tempo\n43\t\n44\t**What it is** — The authors illustrate local muscular endurance with the 400 m: \"It is clear that stride length is maintained with some decrease in tempo at the high-mastery level by means of local strength-endurance. In the fatigued state, athletes of different sex and level of qualification all display a greater decrease in stride length than tempo, but the decrease in speed is associated exclusively with the strength factor.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250.) Fig 4.23 (Popov 1972), \"Change in stride length accompanying increase in the speed of running 400m,\" verified against the page image: the x-axis is 400 m time from 53 s to 46 s (faster to the right); the left axes give stride length (200-230 cm) and stride rate (3.7-3.9 per second), the right axis number of strides (170-200). The stride-length line rises from about 207 cm at 53 s to about 227 cm at 46 s, slowly at first and steeply from about 48 s. A line labelled stride rate rises to a peak near 50 s and then declines toward 46 s. The label placement makes the number-of-strides line hard to tell apart from the stride-rate line, so no values are read for those two.\n45\t\n46\t**Why it works** — The authors attribute the held stride length to local strength-endurance and the loss of speed under fatigue \"exclusively\" to the strength factor; they give no further mechanism or data beyond the figure.\n47\t\n48\t**When to apply** — For 400 m and similar speed-endurance events: treat the ability to keep stride length late in the race as a strength-endurance target, and use special strength exercises in the event's regime to build it (the authors' general prescription for local endurance, same page). Eric's baseline: \"Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 96). Inference: for football, repeated sprints over a drive are much shorter than 400 m, so this claim fits defensive backs' or receivers' long-run fatigue only loosely.\n49\t\n50\t**When NOT to apply** — \"Exclusively with the strength factor\" is a strong statement resting on one cited data set (Popov 1972) whose athletes, sample and method are not described. The figure's x-axis is 400 m race time, not time within a race. Inference: it therefore plots runners (or performances) of different speeds, not fatigue within one race, so it does not itself show the fatigue claim; the caption does not say whether the data are cross-sectional or longitudinal. Inference: stride rate also declines at the fastest times in the figure, so the figure alone does not rule out a frequency contribution.\n51\t\n52\t**Example** — The book's own: Fig 4.23, where faster 400 m times come with longer strides and, at the fastest times, a slight fall in stride rate (Popov 1972; PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n53\t\n54\t**Related** — [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]], [[claim--three-zone-force-velocity-curve-for-cyclic-locomotion]].\n55\t=================== claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport.md\n56\t---\n57\ttype: library-entry\n58\tkind: claim\n59\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n60\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n61\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n62\ttopic: strength training for endurance athletes\n63\tcategory: [endurance, concurrent-training, strength-endurance, distance-sport]\n64\tconfidence: contested\n65\tingest-tier: full-text\n66\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t# The Authors Claim Strength Training Raises Haemoglobin and Myoglobin More Than Endurance Training and Helps Distance Sports More Than Aerobic Training\n70\t\n71\t**What it is** — Under the subheading \"Strength Training and General Endurance,\" the authors cite \"research showing that strength training leads to a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, 1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of training, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & Filin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981)\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251, verified against the page images). \"Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local (strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport\" (seven citations, 1962-1974), \"but in our time, it has not been given serious attention,\" because endurance methods aimed \"chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by distance training)\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251).\n72\t\n73\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism in this passage. Inference: it follows from their preceding argument that endurance depends on the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities (see Related), but they do not state that link here.\n74\t\n75\t**When to apply** — As a reason to keep strength training in endurance and repeat-effort athletes' programs rather than replacing it with more aerobic volume. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182). Inference: this claim runs the other way from the usual interference concern: here strength is said to help endurance, not endurance to harm strength.\n76\t\n77\t**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: one citation does not appear to support the sentence it is attached to. The published title of Pattengale & Holloszy (Am J Physiol 1967;213(3):783-785) is \"Augmentation of skeletal muscle myoglobin by a program of treadmill running\": a running (endurance) program, not strength training. Only the title and journal listing were checked, not the paper's content, so the book's comparison of strength and endurance training cannot be confirmed from that source. The other citations are Soviet or older, not described in the text, and not in the library. \"Contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" gives no sport, measure or size of effect. In this library, the Ch1 entry on circuit training and Issurin's concurrent-training case study describe the opposite direction of interaction (endurance work compromising strength), which does not contradict this claim but limits how it combines with endurance volume.\n78\t\n79\t**Example** — The book gives no worked example. Inference: a cross-country runner who adds two heavy strength sessions a week in the base phase, instead of a fourth easy run, is the kind of change this claim argues for.\n80\t\n81\t**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1.12, endurance work compromising strength), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]] (Issurin, interference in the other direction).\n82\t=================== principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them.md\n83\t---\n84\ttype: library-entry\n85\tkind: principle\n86\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n87\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n88\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n89\ttopic: motor and cardiorespiratory interaction\n90\tcategory: [endurance, conditioning, programming, special-physical-preparation]\n91\tconfidence: supported\n92\tingest-tier: full-text\n93\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n94\t---\n95\t\n96\t# General Endurance Is the Joint Product of the Cardiorespiratory and Motor Systems, with the Motor System in the Lead; Training Them Apart Is an Error\n97\t\n98\t**What it is** — The authors treat endurance as \"a complex resultant of motor abilities\" (six citations) and define general endurance \"in terms of the cardiovascular-respiratory functions which provide the necessary oxygen and the appropriate state of the neuromuscular system.\" So endurance development \"should be carried out in the form of a scheme of training complexes based on the interaction between the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems.\" This agrees, they say, with \"the motor-visceral reflexes (Mogendovich, 1962) and the energetics of muscle contraction (Arshavsky, 1967),\" under which \"the efficiency of muscular work is determined by the interdependence of motor activity, topography, sequentiality and the work regime of the muscles involved, and the respiratory, circulatory and metabolic processes in the body.\" Of the two, the motor processes play \"the key role insofar as functioning of the motor apparatus determines the training of the cardiovascular-respiratory systems.\" This cooperation \"is important for improving work-capacity, not only in endurance sports, but in all sports. Therefore, it is a serious error to draw a line between them, as is quite common in the methodology of special physical training.\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251, verified against the page image.)\n99\t\n100\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning rests on the cited Soviet concepts of motor-visceral reflexes and muscle-contraction energetics: the muscles' work sets the demand the heart and lungs adapt to. They give no data here.\n101\t\n102\t**When to apply** — When designing conditioning: build sessions in which the sport's movements and work regimes drive the cardiorespiratory load (training complexes), rather than separate \"lifting\" and \"conditioning\" blocks with no shared movement content. Eric's baseline: \"'Everything we do is some form of conditioning': lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system ... and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122). Inference: this is close agreement; the book adds the claim that the motor system leads.\n103\t\n104\t**When NOT to apply** — The \"training complexes\" are not specified in this passage (no exercises, durations or intensities), so the principle gives a direction without a method. The supporting concepts (Mogendovich 1962, Arshavsky 1967) are Soviet physiology not described in the text and not in the library. Inference: it does not forbid separate aerobic work; it criticises treating the two systems as unrelated.\n105\t\n106\t**Example** — The book gives no worked example here. Inference: a lineman conditioning circuit built from stance starts, short drives and sled pushes at a set work:rest ratio trains the motor and cardiorespiratory demands together, as against separate gassers and a separate lift.\n107\t\n108\t**Related** — [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--s-factors-pyramidal-fitness-model]].\n109\t=================== framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness.md\n110\t---\n111\ttype: library-entry\n112\tkind: framework\n113\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n114\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n115\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n116\ttopic: functional specialisation over years\n117\tcategory: [long-term-development, special-physical-preparation, cross-training, specificity]\n118\tconfidence: supported\n119\tingest-tier: full-text\n120\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n121\t---\n122\t\n123\t# Functional Specialisation in Four Levels: General Abilities, Coordination, Explosive Force and Specific Endurance, Then the Structure of Special Physical Preparedness\n124\t\n125\t**What it is** — Under \"The Process of Functional Specialisation,\" the authors give \"an ascending flowchart\" of how the body specialises \"over many years of training\" (Fig 4.24, verified against the page image; PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251):\n126\t- **Level 1 (generalised abilities):** speed, strength and endurance, the \"flexible mechanisms of coordination, regulation and energy production\" that serve daily life and \"offer extensive possibilities for adaptation.\"\n127\t- **Level 2:** coordination. The demands of sport \"stimulate the motor function, which coordinates all muscular activity.\"\n128\t- **Level 3:** the ability to display explosive force, and specific endurance. \"The motor function specialises chiefly by developing the ability to display explosive force and specific endurance independent of the type of sport.\" Specific endurance \"may be predominantly cardiovascular or local muscular in nature,\" and explosive force relates to starting strength, acceleration-strength and absolute strength (3.2.1).\n129\t- **Level 4 (specialised abilities):** the structure of special physical preparedness, formed in \"the intensive stage of functional specialisation,\" which \"displays its own form with respect to interaction of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems.\"\n130\t\n131\tWithin these structures the functional systems relate by **cooperation** (\"effective interaction of all the systems\") or **subordination** (\"the productivity of one system is enhanced by the functional potentials of the others\"). \"The structure of special physical preparedness is determined by the specific training regime, and does not relate to conditions imposed by other sporting activities. This fact should be noted by anyone who may be contemplating the use of generalised cross training with various sports.\" (PDF pp. 262-263 / printed pp. 251-252, verified against the page images.)\n132\t\n133\t**Why it works** — The authors present it as a description of the general sequence, not a tested model; the only mechanism given is that sporting demands stimulate the motor function, which then specialises. No data are attached to the flowchart.\n134\t\n135\t**When to apply** — As a long-term development map: build general speed, strength and endurance and coordination first, then explosive force and specific endurance, then the sport's own structure of preparedness. Use the cross-training warning when another sport is proposed as preparation: the authors say it does not build the target sport's special preparedness. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" is the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101). Inference: both are bottom-up hierarchies that end in sport-specific ability; the Vermeil sequence orders physical qualities, while this flowchart puts coordination below explosive force and specific endurance.\n136\t\n137\t**When NOT to apply** — \"Independent of the type of sport\" at Level 3 and \"does not relate to conditions imposed by other sporting activities\" at Level 4 pull in different directions: Level 3 is general across sports, Level 4 is not. The text gives no ages, durations or criteria for moving between levels. The cross-training statement is a strong claim without cited data; it addresses special preparedness, not general fitness or recovery uses of other sports.\n138\t\n139\t**Example** — The book gives no worked example for the flowchart; its factor-analysis tables for high jumpers and skaters (see Related) are offered as data on the Level 4 structure.\n140\t\n141\t**Related** — [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]] (Ch2.3.4), [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]] (Ch2.3.1; agreement), [[framework--four-component-model-of-explosive-force]] (Ch3.2.1), [[framework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness]] (Ch1.5).\n142\t=================== case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\n143\t---\n144\ttype: library-entry\n145\tkind: case-study\n146\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n147\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n148\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n149\ttopic: high jumper preparedness factor structure\n150\tcategory: [jumping, factor-analysis, isometrics, athlete-development]\n151\tconfidence: supported\n152\tingest-tier: full-text\n153\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n154\t---\n155\t\n156\t# High Jumpers (Nikitin 1983): As Qualification Rises, Isometric Knee-Extension Power Moves Up and Plantarflexor Power Moves Down the Factor Structure\n157\t\n158\t**What it is** — The authors present factor analysis as a way to study the structure of special physical preparedness, \"especially informative\" when it compares athletes of different qualification or one athlete at different stages; data from many sports show \"distinct changes in the structure of an athlete's physical preparedness with increasing sports proficiency\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252). Table 4.1 (Nikitin 1983), \"The factor structure (in %) of Special Physical Preparedness of high jumpers, in terms of the major factors displayed by a large sample of competitors of different levels of qualification,\" verified against the page image:\n159\t\n160\t| Factor | Class 2-3 athletes | % | Class 1 - MS | % |\n161\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n162\t| I | General athletic preparedness | 29.6 | Specific athletic preparedness | 27.4 |\n163\t| II | Power of plantarflexors | 19.6 | Power of knee extension isometrically | 23.4 |\n164\t| III | Power of knee extension in dynamic regime | 13.0 | Power of knee extension dynamically | 18.5 |\n165\t| IV | Isometric strength of knee extensors | 10.5 | Power of plantarflexors | 11.8 |\n166\t| V | Explosive-strength of knee extensors (isometric) | 6.5 | (none listed) | |\n167\t\n168\tThe authors' reading: higher-class jumpers develop the specific ability to generate powerful take-off force (factor I) and powerful body extension with the knee and back extensors (factor III); explosive force of the take-off leg's extensors under isometric conditions (factor II) \"assumes major significance,\" growing from what were factors IV and V in Class 2-3; plantarflexor indicators move from factor II to factor IV, \"the contribution of which diminishes significantly\"; and \"the role of special physical preparedness of jumpers increases with level of qualification.\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252.)\n169\t\n170\t**Why it works** — Here the authors do give a mechanism. Isometric strength matters at high level \"due to the large loading of the muscles during the take-off, where the support (take-off) leg acts as a lever, and converts into vertical lift the angular velocity acquired during the run-up.\" Plantarflexors fade \"because under intense overloading the muscles of the support leg are already unable to make a significant contribution to the dynamics of the take-off.\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252.)\n171\t\n172\t**When to apply** — For jumpers and athletes whose key action is a planted, high-load take-off: as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power. More generally, the authors' rule is to plan from \"the general trends of change in the factor structure ... with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253). Eric's baseline: isometric mid-thigh pull peak force enters the DSI used to choose between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76). Inference: these data are a reason to keep an isometric knee-extension measure for jumpers, but they say nothing about the DSI ratio itself.\n173\t\n174\t**When NOT to apply** — The sample is described only as \"a large sample\"; the number, factor-extraction method and test battery are not given. The percentages do not sum to 100 (about 79% for Class 2-3 and 81% for Class 1-MS), and the text does not say what the remainder is. The labels changing between columns (\"general\" to \"specific\" athletic preparedness) mean factors are not the same variables across groups, so the shifts are interpretive. This is high-jump data; Inference: transfer to other take-off actions (a cut, a block) is not shown.\n175\t\n176\t**Example** — The book's own: Table 4.1 above.\n177\t\n178\t**Related** — [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive]] (4.2.4, explosive isometrics, which the text cross-references), [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]] (Ch2.2.4; agreement on qualification-dependent order), [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--correlation-methodology-limits-and-tie-in-effect]] (limits of correlation-based structure studies).\n179\t=================== case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\n180\t---\n181\ttype: library-entry\n182\tkind: case-study\n183\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n184\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n185\tchapter: \"4.4\"\n186\ttopic: skater special fitness factor structure\n187\tcategory: [endurance, factor-analysis, technique, athlete-development]\n188\tconfidence: supported\n189\tingest-tier: full-text\n190\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n191\t---\n192\t\n193\t# Skaters (Shchirkovyets and Rosovtsyev 1977): Aerobic Factors Dominate at Class 1, Energetic Efficiency and Technique Dominate at Master of Sport\n194\t\n195\t**What it is** — Table 4.2 (Shchirkovyets & Rosovtsyev 1977), \"The factor structure (in %) of special fitness of skaters in terms of the major factors displayed by a large sample of competitors of different levels of qualification,\" verified against the page image (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253):\n196\t\n197\t| Factor | Class 1 athletes | % | Master of Sport | % |\n198\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n199\t| I | Aerobic power | 35.9 | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 36.5 |\n200\t| II | Aerobic capacity | 23.3 | Multi-faceted technical preparedness | 21.9 |\n201\t| III | Power of anaerobic processes | 16.8 | Power of anaerobic processes | 19.8 |\n202\t| IV | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 2.7 | Aerobic efficiency | 10.9 |\n203\t| V | Multi-faceted technical preparedness | 8.9 | (none listed) | |\n204\t\n205\tThe authors note \"the sharp rise in the role of energetic efficiency of special work and multifaceted technical preparedness\"; that Class 1's first two factors \"may be combined as one factor (i.e. aerobic efficiency) whose role at Master of Sport level decreases\"; and that \"the significance of anaerobic power increases slightly (factor III)\" (PDF pp. 263-264 / printed pp. 252-253). Their conclusion: \"a high level of aerobic potential still does not ensure top-level performance in skating. To efficiently generate a high level of aerobic power, it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity.\" Factor research gives \"a useful statistical model\" of special physical preparedness, whose form depends on the individual's motor qualities and the organisation of training, so programming must consider general trends with rising proficiency, individual peculiarities and changes in the main functional characteristics (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253).\n206\t\n207\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the statement that technique and anaerobic productivity are needed to use aerobic power efficiently.\n208\t\n209\t**When to apply** — When an athlete's aerobic markers are already high but performance stalls: shift emphasis toward economy of the specific work, technical preparedness and anaerobic power, as the Master of Sport structure suggests. Eric's baseline: \"Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 172). Inference: the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\n210\t\n211\t**When NOT to apply** — The sample is \"a large sample\" with no number, method or test battery given. The columns do not sum to 100 (about 88% and 89%), and the remainder is not explained. Factor labels differ between columns, so the comparison is interpretive. The conclusion that a \"high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" is necessary conflicts with the authors' earlier advice in 4.4 to \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); see [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]].\n212\t\n213\t**Example** — The book's own: Table 4.2 above.\n214\t\n215\t**Related** — [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]] (Ch3; agreement on skill and economy).\n216\t"}]},"uuid":"80f4ed8b-ad9c-47af-bb95-f8080d9b8c90","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:40:44.501Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/0949803b-b5a4-4eec-8bc8-87b3ccc4c359/scratchpad/e3.txt","content":"=================== principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: local specificity of endurance adaptation\ncategory: [endurance, specificity, conditioning, cross-training]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Anaerobic and Local Endurance Adaptations Stay in the Muscles That Were Trained: General Conditioning Raises VO2 Max but Not Sport-Specific Work Capacity\n\n**What it is** — \"Sports exercises are performed by specific muscle groups and if metabolic processes in these muscles are especially intense, then the products of anaerobic metabolism accumulate, resulting in fatigue and decreased work capacity. Therefore, adaptation of the muscles to anaerobic work is clearly local in nature.\" Training different muscle groups can give \"approximately equivalent functional effect at the level of the cardiovascular-respiratory systems,\" but \"it is displayed only in specific situations, i.e. while using those same muscle groups (Clausen et al, 1970; Holmer & Astrand, 1972).\" For skiers, \"running and imitation skiing without poles raises the general functional level during the preparatory period, including VO2 max, but does not develop the specific physical preparedness necessary for speed of movement on skis,\" whereas \"imitation skiing and indoor skiing on rollers with poles enhances local muscular endurance and vesicular reactions in the muscles of the upper extremities, which leads to the efficient redistribution of blood flow and an increase in specific work-capacity for skiing (Yevstratov et al, 1975).\" The authors define local muscular endurance as \"the athlete's ability to produce the strength component of movement for a prolonged period without significant fatigue,\" and say \"practical ways of developing local muscular endurance still need serious research\"; \"one should find ways to intensify muscular work in those regimes characterising the given sport and utilise special strength exercises for this purpose (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250, verified against the page image.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: fatigue products build up in the specific muscles doing intense work, and the blood-flow and local endurance adaptations develop in those same muscles, so a central gain trained with other muscles does not carry the local part.\n\n**When to apply** — When choosing conditioning modes: if the sport's limiting fatigue is local (specific muscle groups under repeated intense effort), condition with the sport's muscles in the sport's regime, using special strength exercises, not only general running or cycling. Eric's baseline: \"'Everything we do is some form of conditioning': lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system ... and should be classified and counted as conditioning.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122). Inference: this principle supports counting position-specific drills as conditioning, because general running would not build the same local capacity.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors grant that general work still raises the general functional level and VO2 max in the preparatory period; this is not an argument to drop it. The principle concerns local (anaerobic, muscular) endurance; the cardiorespiratory gain from other muscle groups is described as roughly equivalent. The citations are summarised without data.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: skiers who run or imitate skiing without poles (VO2 max rises, ski speed does not) against skiers who roller-ski with poles (upper-limb local endurance and specific work capacity rise) (Yevstratov et al 1975; PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]] (the authors' caution on generalised cross training), [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, general versus local strength-endurance), [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (metabolism and fatigue as specificity dimensions).\n=================== claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: 400 m stride length and strength-endurance\ncategory: [sprinting, strength-endurance, fatigue, speed-endurance]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Faster 400 m Runners Hold Stride Length Through Local Strength-Endurance; Under Fatigue, Stride Length Falls More Than Tempo\n\n**What it is** — The authors illustrate local muscular endurance with the 400 m: \"It is clear that stride length is maintained with some decrease in tempo at the high-mastery level by means of local strength-endurance. In the fatigued state, athletes of different sex and level of qualification all display a greater decrease in stride length than tempo, but the decrease in speed is associated exclusively with the strength factor.\" (PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250.) Fig 4.23 (Popov 1972), \"Change in stride length accompanying increase in the speed of running 400m,\" verified against the page image: the x-axis is 400 m time from 53 s to 46 s (faster to the right); the left axes give stride length (200-230 cm) and stride rate (3.7-3.9 per second), the right axis number of strides (170-200). The stride-length line rises from about 207 cm at 53 s to about 227 cm at 46 s, slowly at first and steeply from about 48 s. A line labelled stride rate rises to a peak near 50 s and then declines toward 46 s. The label placement makes the number-of-strides line hard to tell apart from the stride-rate line, so no values are read for those two.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute the held stride length to local strength-endurance and the loss of speed under fatigue \"exclusively\" to the strength factor; they give no further mechanism or data beyond the figure.\n\n**When to apply** — For 400 m and similar speed-endurance events: treat the ability to keep stride length late in the race as a strength-endurance target, and use special strength exercises in the event's regime to build it (the authors' general prescription for local endurance, same page). Eric's baseline: \"Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 96). Inference: for football, repeated sprints over a drive are much shorter than 400 m, so this claim fits defensive backs' or receivers' long-run fatigue only loosely.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Exclusively with the strength factor\" is a strong statement resting on one cited data set (Popov 1972) whose athletes, sample and method are not described. The figure's x-axis is 400 m race time, not time within a race. Inference: it therefore plots runners (or performances) of different speeds, not fatigue within one race, so it does not itself show the fatigue claim; the caption does not say whether the data are cross-sectional or longitudinal. Inference: stride rate also declines at the fastest times in the figure, so the figure alone does not rule out a frequency contribution.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: Fig 4.23, where faster 400 m times come with longer strides and, at the fastest times, a slight fall in stride rate (Popov 1972; PDF p. 261 / printed p. 250).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]], [[claim--three-zone-force-velocity-curve-for-cyclic-locomotion]].\n=================== claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: strength training for endurance athletes\ncategory: [endurance, concurrent-training, strength-endurance, distance-sport]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Authors Claim Strength Training Raises Haemoglobin and Myoglobin More Than Endurance Training and Helps Distance Sports More Than Aerobic Training\n\n**What it is** — Under the subheading \"Strength Training and General Endurance,\" the authors cite \"research showing that strength training leads to a greater rise in haemoglobin content (Petrov & Lapchenkov, 1978) and myoglobin (Pattengale & Holloszy, 1967; Hemmingsen, 1963) than endurance training; and that strength training, even in the beginning stages of training, contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training (Neminushchii & Filin, 1972; Gavrilenko & Mikhailov, 1981)\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251, verified against the page images). \"Several decades ago, scientists pointed out the importance of local (strength) endurance for cyclic types of sport\" (seven citations, 1962-1974), \"but in our time, it has not been given serious attention,\" because endurance methods aimed \"chiefly at improving the cardiovascular-respiratory functions (mainly by distance training)\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism in this passage. Inference: it follows from their preceding argument that endurance depends on the muscles' strength and oxidative qualities (see Related), but they do not state that link here.\n\n**When to apply** — As a reason to keep strength training in endurance and repeat-effort athletes' programs rather than replacing it with more aerobic volume. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript + slides)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182). Inference: this claim runs the other way from the usual interference concern: here strength is said to help endurance, not endurance to harm strength.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Why `contested`: one citation does not appear to support the sentence it is attached to. The published title of Pattengale & Holloszy (Am J Physiol 1967;213(3):783-785) is \"Augmentation of skeletal muscle myoglobin by a program of treadmill running\": a running (endurance) program, not strength training. Only the title and journal listing were checked, not the paper's content, so the book's comparison of strength and endurance training cannot be confirmed from that source. The other citations are Soviet or older, not described in the text, and not in the library. \"Contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" gives no sport, measure or size of effect. In this library, the Ch1 entry on circuit training and Issurin's concurrent-training case study describe the opposite direction of interaction (endurance work compromising strength), which does not contradict this claim but limits how it combines with endurance volume.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no worked example. Inference: a cross-country runner who adds two heavy strength sessions a week in the base phase, instead of a fourth easy run, is the kind of change this claim argues for.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1.12, endurance work compromising strength), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]] (Issurin, interference in the other direction).\n=================== principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: motor and cardiorespiratory interaction\ncategory: [endurance, conditioning, programming, special-physical-preparation]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# General Endurance Is the Joint Product of the Cardiorespiratory and Motor Systems, with the Motor System in the Lead; Training Them Apart Is an Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors treat endurance as \"a complex resultant of motor abilities\" (six citations) and define general endurance \"in terms of the cardiovascular-respiratory functions which provide the necessary oxygen and the appropriate state of the neuromuscular system.\" So endurance development \"should be carried out in the form of a scheme of training complexes based on the interaction between the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems.\" This agrees, they say, with \"the motor-visceral reflexes (Mogendovich, 1962) and the energetics of muscle contraction (Arshavsky, 1967),\" under which \"the efficiency of muscular work is determined by the interdependence of motor activity, topography, sequentiality and the work regime of the muscles involved, and the respiratory, circulatory and metabolic processes in the body.\" Of the two, the motor processes play \"the key role insofar as functioning of the motor apparatus determines the training of the cardiovascular-respiratory systems.\" This cooperation \"is important for improving work-capacity, not only in endurance sports, but in all sports. Therefore, it is a serious error to draw a line between them, as is quite common in the methodology of special physical training.\" (PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251, verified against the page image.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning rests on the cited Soviet concepts of motor-visceral reflexes and muscle-contraction energetics: the muscles' work sets the demand the heart and lungs adapt to. They give no data here.\n\n**When to apply** — When designing conditioning: build sessions in which the sport's movements and work regimes drive the cardiorespiratory load (training complexes), rather than separate \"lifting\" and \"conditioning\" blocks with no shared movement content. Eric's baseline: \"'Everything we do is some form of conditioning': lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system ... and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it.\" **Explicit** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122). Inference: this is close agreement; the book adds the claim that the motor system leads.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The \"training complexes\" are not specified in this passage (no exercises, durations or intensities), so the principle gives a direction without a method. The supporting concepts (Mogendovich 1962, Arshavsky 1967) are Soviet physiology not described in the text and not in the library. Inference: it does not forbid separate aerobic work; it criticises treating the two systems as unrelated.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no worked example here. Inference: a lineman conditioning circuit built from stance starts, short drives and sled pushes at a set work:rest ratio trains the motor and cardiorespiratory demands together, as against separate gassers and a separate lift.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[framework--s-factors-pyramidal-fitness-model]].\n=================== framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: functional specialisation over years\ncategory: [long-term-development, special-physical-preparation, cross-training, specificity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Functional Specialisation in Four Levels: General Abilities, Coordination, Explosive Force and Specific Endurance, Then the Structure of Special Physical Preparedness\n\n**What it is** — Under \"The Process of Functional Specialisation,\" the authors give \"an ascending flowchart\" of how the body specialises \"over many years of training\" (Fig 4.24, verified against the page image; PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251):\n- **Level 1 (generalised abilities):** speed, strength and endurance, the \"flexible mechanisms of coordination, regulation and energy production\" that serve daily life and \"offer extensive possibilities for adaptation.\"\n- **Level 2:** coordination. The demands of sport \"stimulate the motor function, which coordinates all muscular activity.\"\n- **Level 3:** the ability to display explosive force, and specific endurance. \"The motor function specialises chiefly by developing the ability to display explosive force and specific endurance independent of the type of sport.\" Specific endurance \"may be predominantly cardiovascular or local muscular in nature,\" and explosive force relates to starting strength, acceleration-strength and absolute strength (3.2.1).\n- **Level 4 (specialised abilities):** the structure of special physical preparedness, formed in \"the intensive stage of functional specialisation,\" which \"displays its own form with respect to interaction of the cardiovascular-respiratory and motor systems.\"\n\nWithin these structures the functional systems relate by **cooperation** (\"effective interaction of all the systems\") or **subordination** (\"the productivity of one system is enhanced by the functional potentials of the others\"). \"The structure of special physical preparedness is determined by the specific training regime, and does not relate to conditions imposed by other sporting activities. This fact should be noted by anyone who may be contemplating the use of generalised cross training with various sports.\" (PDF pp. 262-263 / printed pp. 251-252, verified against the page images.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present it as a description of the general sequence, not a tested model; the only mechanism given is that sporting demands stimulate the motor function, which then specialises. No data are attached to the flowchart.\n\n**When to apply** — As a long-term development map: build general speed, strength and endurance and coordination first, then explosive force and specific endurance, then the sport's own structure of preparedness. Use the cross-training warning when another sport is proposed as preparation: the authors say it does not build the target sport's special preparedness. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" is the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 101). Inference: both are bottom-up hierarchies that end in sport-specific ability; the Vermeil sequence orders physical qualities, while this flowchart puts coordination below explosive force and specific endurance.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Independent of the type of sport\" at Level 3 and \"does not relate to conditions imposed by other sporting activities\" at Level 4 pull in different directions: Level 3 is general across sports, Level 4 is not. The text gives no ages, durations or criteria for moving between levels. The cross-training statement is a strong claim without cited data; it addresses special preparedness, not general fitness or recovery uses of other sports.\n\n**Example** — The book gives no worked example for the flowchart; its factor-analysis tables for high jumpers and skaters (see Related) are offered as data on the Level 4 structure.\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]] (Ch2.3.4), [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]] (Ch2.3.1; agreement), [[framework--four-component-model-of-explosive-force]] (Ch3.2.1), [[framework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness]] (Ch1.5).\n=================== case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: high jumper preparedness factor structure\ncategory: [jumping, factor-analysis, isometrics, athlete-development]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# High Jumpers (Nikitin 1983): As Qualification Rises, Isometric Knee-Extension Power Moves Up and Plantarflexor Power Moves Down the Factor Structure\n\n**What it is** — The authors present factor analysis as a way to study the structure of special physical preparedness, \"especially informative\" when it compares athletes of different qualification or one athlete at different stages; data from many sports show \"distinct changes in the structure of an athlete's physical preparedness with increasing sports proficiency\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252). Table 4.1 (Nikitin 1983), \"The factor structure (in %) of Special Physical Preparedness of high jumpers, in terms of the major factors displayed by a large sample of competitors of different levels of qualification,\" verified against the page image:\n\n| Factor | Class 2-3 athletes | % | Class 1 - MS | % |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| I | General athletic preparedness | 29.6 | Specific athletic preparedness | 27.4 |\n| II | Power of plantarflexors | 19.6 | Power of knee extension isometrically | 23.4 |\n| III | Power of knee extension in dynamic regime | 13.0 | Power of knee extension dynamically | 18.5 |\n| IV | Isometric strength of knee extensors | 10.5 | Power of plantarflexors | 11.8 |\n| V | Explosive-strength of knee extensors (isometric) | 6.5 | (none listed) | |\n\nThe authors' reading: higher-class jumpers develop the specific ability to generate powerful take-off force (factor I) and powerful body extension with the knee and back extensors (factor III); explosive force of the take-off leg's extensors under isometric conditions (factor II) \"assumes major significance,\" growing from what were factors IV and V in Class 2-3; plantarflexor indicators move from factor II to factor IV, \"the contribution of which diminishes significantly\"; and \"the role of special physical preparedness of jumpers increases with level of qualification.\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252.)\n\n**Why it works** — Here the authors do give a mechanism. Isometric strength matters at high level \"due to the large loading of the muscles during the take-off, where the support (take-off) leg acts as a lever, and converts into vertical lift the angular velocity acquired during the run-up.\" Plantarflexors fade \"because under intense overloading the muscles of the support leg are already unable to make a significant contribution to the dynamics of the take-off.\" (PDF p. 263 / printed p. 252.)\n\n**When to apply** — For jumpers and athletes whose key action is a planted, high-load take-off: as qualification rises, give more weight to isometric and explosive-isometric knee-extensor strength in the take-off position and less to plantarflexor power. More generally, the authors' rule is to plan from \"the general trends of change in the factor structure ... with increase in proficiency, as well as the individual peculiarities of a specific athlete\" (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253). Eric's baseline: isometric mid-thigh pull peak force enters the DSI used to choose between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76). Inference: these data are a reason to keep an isometric knee-extension measure for jumpers, but they say nothing about the DSI ratio itself.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The sample is described only as \"a large sample\"; the number, factor-extraction method and test battery are not given. The percentages do not sum to 100 (about 79% for Class 2-3 and 81% for Class 1-MS), and the text does not say what the remainder is. The labels changing between columns (\"general\" to \"specific\" athletic preparedness) mean factors are not the same variables across groups, so the shifts are interpretive. This is high-jump data; Inference: transfer to other take-off actions (a cut, a block) is not shown.\n\n**Example** — The book's own: Table 4.1 above.\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--isometric-contraction-categorization-voluntary-reflexive]] (4.2.4, explosive isometrics, which the text cross-references), [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]] (Ch2.2.4; agreement on qualification-dependent order), [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--correlation-methodology-limits-and-tie-in-effect]] (limits of correlation-based structure studies).\n=================== case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.4\"\ntopic: skater special fitness factor structure\ncategory: [endurance, factor-analysis, technique, athlete-development]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Skaters (Shchirkovyets and Rosovtsyev 1977): Aerobic Factors Dominate at Class 1, Energetic Efficiency and Technique Dominate at Master of Sport\n\n**What it is** — Table 4.2 (Shchirkovyets & Rosovtsyev 1977), \"The factor structure (in %) of special fitness of skaters in terms of the major factors displayed by a large sample of competitors of different levels of qualification,\" verified against the page image (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253):\n\n| Factor | Class 1 athletes | % | Master of Sport | % |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| I | Aerobic power | 35.9 | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 36.5 |\n| II | Aerobic capacity | 23.3 | Multi-faceted technical preparedness | 21.9 |\n| III | Power of anaerobic processes | 16.8 | Power of anaerobic processes | 19.8 |\n| IV | Energetic efficiency of executing specific work | 2.7 | Aerobic efficiency | 10.9 |\n| V | Multi-faceted technical preparedness | 8.9 | (none listed) | |\n\nThe authors note \"the sharp rise in the role of energetic efficiency of special work and multifaceted technical preparedness\"; that Class 1's first two factors \"may be combined as one factor (i.e. aerobic efficiency) whose role at Master of Sport level decreases\"; and that \"the significance of anaerobic power increases slightly (factor III)\" (PDF pp. 263-264 / printed pp. 252-253). Their conclusion: \"a high level of aerobic potential still does not ensure top-level performance in skating. To efficiently generate a high level of aerobic power, it [is] necessary to have excellent movement technique and a high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity.\" Factor research gives \"a useful statistical model\" of special physical preparedness, whose form depends on the individual's motor qualities and the organisation of training, so programming must consider general trends with rising proficiency, individual peculiarities and changes in the main functional characteristics (PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the statement that technique and anaerobic productivity are needed to use aerobic power efficiently.\n\n**When to apply** — When an athlete's aerobic markers are already high but performance stalls: shift emphasis toward economy of the specific work, technical preparedness and anaerobic power, as the Master of Sport structure suggests. Eric's baseline: \"Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost.\" Tagged **Inferred (transcript)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 172). Inference: the skaters' jump in \"energetic efficiency of executing specific work\" (2.7% to 36.5%) is consistent with that teaching point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The sample is \"a large sample\" with no number, method or test battery given. The columns do not sum to 100 (about 88% and 89%), and the remainder is not explained. Factor labels differ between columns, so the comparison is interpretive. The conclusion that a \"high level of anaerobic (non-oxidative glycolytic) productivity\" is necessary conflicts with the authors' earlier advice in 4.4 to \"decrease the proportion of glycolysis\" (PDF p. 260 / printed p. 249); see [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]].\n\n**Example** — The book's own: Table 4.2 above.\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]] (Ch3; agreement on skill and economy).\n","numLines":216,"startLine":1,"totalLines":216}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"53cf932f-e3c1-41d5-af1b-cfa73174f9e7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
