{"parentUuid":"2e221214-827c-437b-af5f-3568e1521029","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffUi6SUScyV6KvigJ9Bd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Figures 4.19 and 4.20 match the entry descriptions. Now baseline quotes, link targets, and the p.266 cross-reference."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10727,"cache_read_input_tokens":155706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10727,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffUhuxC4F9tHzRdjN8dN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c7237863-55d2-4241-ba60-68249e6be214","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:41:26.704Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c7237863-55d2-4241-ba60-68249e6be214","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffUi6SUScyV6KvigJ9Bd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011C1q8xv5rNd2oaCw4XNrib","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"B=/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md; for n in 33 74 76 77 78 79 96 97 101 102 103 104 113 116 117 122 123 124 172 182; do echo \"--- $n: $(sed -n ${n}p $B | cut -c1-400)\"; done","description":"Print cited method-baseline lines"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10727,"cache_read_input_tokens":155706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10727,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011C1q8xv5rNd2oaCw4XNrib":{"command":"B=/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md; for n in 33 74 76 77 78 79 96 97 101 102 103 104 113 116 117 122 123 124 172 182; do echo \"--- $n: $(sed -n ${n}p $B | cut -c1-400)\"; done","description":"Print cited method-baseline lines"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffUhuxC4F9tHzRdjN8dN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"86f7012a-45d9-49ae-803b-3c2201ad290d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:41:28.095Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"86f7012a-45d9-49ae-803b-3c2201ad290d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f097ba1e-802d-4788-86d0-92157481e0f4","agentId":"ad35f6feb999bff94","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011C1q8xv5rNd2oaCw4XNrib","type":"tool_result","content":"--- 33: - The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q6): \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)**\n--- 74: - Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n--- 76: - The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q\n--- 77: - The standard CMJ family run on ForceDecks: hands-free squat, squat jump (quarter-squat start, 3s hold), countermovement jump (x3), hop test / repeated CMJ (10 reps total, n=5 pairs), and single-leg CMJ (x3 per leg) — each with a scripted weigh-in, stabilization, and rep protocol. **Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)** [revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n--- 78: - The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks \"Mid-Thigh Pull\" setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position, belt pre-tensioned to a fingertip's gap, 3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps. **Inferred (protocol)** [protocol-iso-seated-calf-raise.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n--- 79: - Metric selection for force-plate reporting follows a disciplined framework: pick 1-2 output metrics (the obvious performance result, e.g. jump height), 1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force), 1-3 strategy metrics (how the athlete achieved it, e.g. phase durations), optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences), and add \"fluffy\" metrics (peak power, average pow\n--- 96: - Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\n--- 97: - Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See\n--- 101: - The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n--- 102: - Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of \"sport specific.\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.13-16; week1-lecture.md pp.32-36]\n--- 103: - The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) \n--- 104: - Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n--- 113: - Conditioning is defined two ways: (1) how well an athlete can meet the energy requirements of their specific sport/activity, and (2) how well they sustain strength, speed, and power output across a full competition. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.22]\n--- 116: - For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the inter\n--- 117: - Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n--- 122: - \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n--- 123: - For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous running ability. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md p.9]\n--- 124: - Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.51; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n--- 172: - Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost — elite athletes (cited examples: LeBron James, Deion Sanders) are described as outlasting and outperforming peers partly through tactical efficiency that lowers the physical output required to achieve the same result. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.8-9]\n--- 182: - Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-tr","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"eb248594-e724-41f7-b776-0eb668abfe1a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:41:28.405Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"--- 33: - The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q6): \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)**\n--- 74: - Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliber